  HardwareHelp_L("Screen 3")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("MSX-Video: V9958", "MSX-Video (V9958)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("First of all a few general notes which seem to apply to all commands:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1) The SX and DX registers are 9 bits wide, at least when the registers are initialized. There might be a 10th bit or a carry bit or something like that which is used for border detection. On screen 5 and screen 8, the highest bit is ignored when a byte or pixel is written to the VRAM or read from the VRAM")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2) The SY and DY registers are 10 bits wide. On screen 7 and 8, the highest bit is ignored when a byte or pixel is written to the VRAM or read from the VRAM")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("3) The NX and NY registers are 10 bits wide. The highest bit of NY is never ignored or clipped of. Not even on screen 7 and 8. And for the NX register: I know that the full range is used for the LINE command, though I'm not sure about the other commands.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4) The byte commands (like HMMV and YMMM) always work on entire bytes. The lowest bits of both the X coordinate (DX, SX) and the width (NX) are simply ignored.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("5) Commands that can step in the X direction, like SRCH, LINE and HMMV, first perform the operation they need to do and then they check if they need to do an additional step. Am I still clear? Maybe a little pseudocode shows what I mean:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("for (y conditions) {")
  HardwareHelp_L("  do {")
  HardwareHelp_L("    some operation on a pixel/byte")
  HardwareHelp_L("    update x parameters (sx/dx/nx)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  } while (x and other conditions)")
  HardwareHelp_L("}")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("6) The end condition for stepping in the X direction is either NX=0 or DX/SX is out of the legal X range for the current screen mode. On screen 5/8: DX/SX<0 or >255 (bit 8 is set). On screen 6/7: DX/SX<0 or >511 (bit 9 or a carry bit or something like that has been set).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("7) When you combine note 1), note 5) and note 6) you get some very interesting behaviours on screen 5 and screen 8. For example for LMMV, when you start with DX=256 and x-direction is to the left, the following will happen in the X-loop: The pixel at x=256 will be set. Since the highest bit is ignored, in reality the pixel at x=0 is set. The x-coordinate is decreased with one and becomes 255. The x-coordinate is checked to see if it is in the legal range. Guess what: it is!. So, the vdp will set the pixel at coordinate x=255, x=254, and so on until NX=0.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("8) Point 7) is also valid for the SRCH command, the LINE command and the HMMV command.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("9) When stepping downwards in the Y-direction, the Y coordinate wraps from 1023 to 0. When stepping up, the command does not wrap from 0 to 1023. In stead, the VDP stops after decreasing from 0 to whatever (call it -1 or 1023 or Carry or whatever you like).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Now that you know these general guidelines, I will explain the details of the commands one by one. I will reference the commands by their official names as I'm more used to the official names. Between brackets I will mention the names which you have used in your source (if there is a difference):")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1) POINT (PGET):")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SX and SY are mapped into the legal range of the screen mode (using an AND mask). The real vdp will most probably NOT apply any AND mask to the coordinates but simply ignore the highest bits on the screenmodes where they are not needed.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2) PSET:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Same story as for POINT")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("3) SRCH:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The main work is done in an X-loop which is following the generic structure I mentioned in note 5). That is: the VDP will fetch the point at (SX, SY), compare it to the search color, abort the loop if the color matches (or mismatches, depending on the search flag), decrease or increase SX (depending on the search direction) and only after that, the vdp will check if the border has been reached. Please notice again that SX will not be masked to the range 0...255 on screen 5 and screen 8! The highest bit will simply be ignored by the address generation logic when fetching the point.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4) LINE:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The well-known incremental line algorithm is used. NX is always used as the major length and NY is always used as the minor length. When VDP[45]&1 is 0 (X-axis is major direction), DX will be increased/decreased each time and DY will be increased/decreased only when sum(minor_direction)>major_direction. When VDP[45]&1 is 1 (Y-axis is major direction), DY will be increased/decreased each time and DX will be increased/decreased only when sum(minor_direction)>major_direction. Please notice that the variable which is used as the sum(minor_direction) has a 10-bits range. This does impact the exact behaviour if somebody has put a larger value in the minor length then in the major length. As usual, DX is NOT masked to a 8 bits range on screen 5 and 8. The end condition for the line algorithm is always: NX=0 or DX is out of range.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("5) HMMV (FFILL):")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This command always works with complete bytes. Which means that for example on screen 5, setting NX=1 is the same as setting NX=0. Or setting DX=7 is the same as DX=6. To make life easy, I always divide NX and DX by the number of pixels per byte in my routine. Then I don't need to worry further about the number of pixels per byte. The real vdp always works with NY in a 10-bits range. For example, when you start with NY=612 on screen 7, 100 lines will be filled-up twice. At least, when you are filling in the downward direction as DY only wraps from 1023 to 0 when going down. The highest bit of DY will simply be ignored at the moment that the real VDP generates the address. Though, as you can not see that a line is drawn twice I say in the emulator that NY=512 when it is initialized with a larger value then 512.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("6) LMMV (FILL):")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Almost same story as for the HMMV command. Only difference is that now I do not limit NY to 512 on screen 7 and 8. With LMMV there can be a difference when you draw a line twice. For example if you are drawing with the XOR mask. At the moment that the line is drawn for the second time, the screen will be changed back to its original state. Please notice that you can really see that happen on a real MSX, as the LMMV command is rather slow. It takes something like 6 interrupts to fill one page of vram with the LMMV command...")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("7) YMMM (HCOPY):")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I'm still working on this one. I have already made a few straightforward testcases of which I assumed that they would pose no problem to the emulator. Unfortunately, they did.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The information provided above was discovered and documented by Alex Wulms/XelaSoft")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Lex Lechz: found also a strange behaviour of VDP read- and write-adresses. As we know read-adresses and write-adresses must be set separately. Independent from this definition these adresses seems to have some sort of connection:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1) If setting a VDP read-adress the write-adress becomes the value+1. This means that setting the VRAM read-adress to 0x35FF sets also the VRAM write-adress to 0x3600.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2) Reading or writing to VRAM increases both - read- and write-adress - of VRAM (fact).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("3) If setting a VDP-write adress the read-address becomes the value-1. This means that setting the VRAM write-address to 0x3600 sets also the VRAM read-adress to 0x35FF (not verified).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4) The behaviour described above is verified on V9938 and V9958 video-processors. I don't know if the TMS9918-video processor has the same behaviour or handles read-/write-addresses in a different way.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The described behaviour is very strange and can also interpreted in another way, which is not verified yet: (a) setting the VRAM read-adress saves this address as single read/write-address; (b) setting the VRAM write-adress saves the adress-value -1 as single read/write-adress; (c) reading a byte from VRAM reads from the specified address and increments the current address - where (d) writing a byte to VRAM increments the address first and then writes the byte to the new address.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This really strange behaviour of VRAM can be verified by running the " + Chr(34) + "Fony Demo Disk 1" + Chr(34) + " on MSX-emulations. Emulators which are taking care of the described behaviour will display the graphics correctly, where other Emulators are corrupting the screen-display (smashing the contents of screen 5 and 8, where sprite-animation should be displayed).")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Comportamento do VDP - VDP-behaviour", "MSX-Video (V9958)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("F-nano2 had problems while executed on RuMSX prior V0.40.")
  HardwareHelp_L("This are:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("- Strange behaviour of original VDP in screen 3 using high VRAM area (.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Cause:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("VDP in screen 3 has another undocumented behaviour, which is the reason that this game does not display it's graphics properly.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Technical notes:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("K-KAZ playtested RuMSX 0.12b with his F-NANO2' and returned following comments:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Most important suggestions are:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1. Refer to value of VDP#23 when displaying sprites in screen 3")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2. Real MSX has a bug which is not noted in specification books, and F-nano2 relies on it.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I have seen Fnano2' screen with RuMSX 0.12b.. Though the display is almost as garbles, assignments of screen-modes are proper upper quarter is screen 4, and lower half is screen 3. Furthermore, even if a small racing-course map (something on upper-left) is displayed! Amazing.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("As far as I can see, it is a matter of time before I can see my game exactly being emulated.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("About background in screen 5, it looks OK at first sight, but if you look at it carefully, you'll find they are little improper. (However, I admit they are double-scrolling for real!)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Firstly, about screen 3 area. Sprites are displayed too high. I guess RuMSX is not refering value of VDP#23 for screen 3 when it displays sprite.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I cannot understand why area displayed in screen 3 is incorrect., but do find fragments of colors which are supposed to appear in screen contained in the garbled screen 3 display.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Therefore my opinion, you are not properly emulating pattern name table.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Also about screen 4 area, I can see a number " + Chr(34) + "3" + Chr(34) + " filling it up. So, I think pattern generator table is working properly. Pattern name table must be causing problem.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("About reasons of these, I assume, because I programmed F-nano2' with bug of VDP in real MSX in mind. And since you emulated MSX to act exactly as stated in specification books, my game is not working properly on RuMSX.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I explain, in game screen of F-nano2', over all of ist screen, value of VDP#2 (pattern name table address) is 01111111B. This is supposed to specify page 3 (018000H) in screen 5 mode, and in screen 3 or 4, this is supposed to specify 01FC00H.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("However, in this state, real VDP understands pattern name table for screen 3 or 4, as 017C00H. I don't know the reason. Biggest possiblity: when you are using horizontal scroll facility of V9958, when MSX is displaying an odd-number page, it appears on scroll concatenated to even-number page at it's side (if value of horizontal scroll is 0 then even number page appears on screen). An I think this behaviour is affecting screen 3 or 4, thereby causing MSX to read what is supposed to be 01FC00H as 017C00H. This is, after all, only an estimate.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I suppose, if you correct this glitch, and sprite display position, RuMSX can display F-nano2' screen properly...")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("There are too many unknown facts about MSX.")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("VDP Screen 3 - SCREEN 3", "MSX-Video (V9958)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("Extra information about VRAM usage in different graphic screens.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("First to illustrate this problem, run the following program on an MSX computer.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("10 screen 8:set page 1,1:cls")
  HardwareHelp_L("20 line (0,0)-(15,15),15,bf")
  HardwareHelp_L("30 screen 5:set page 1,1")
  HardwareHelp_L("40 a$=input$(1):' keypress")
  HardwareHelp_L("50 set page 3,3")
  HardwareHelp_L("60 a$=input$(1):' keypress")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("As you can see this program draws a white filled square on page 1 of screen 8. After that screen 5 is displayed, first page 1, after a keypress page 3. What you will on screen 5, page 1 and page 3, is 8 vertical stripes.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Screen 8 | Screen 5")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("So apparently page 1 of screen 8 is stored at the same absolute location as the page 1 and page 3 of screen 5 together.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Screen 7:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When in screen 7 the first byte of VRAM contains the first two pixels (0,0) and (1,0) of page 0. And the second byte contains the following two pixels (2,0) and (3,0) of page 0. This is exacly want you would expect, so nothing strange so far.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Screen 8:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When in screen 8 the first byte of VRAM contains the first pixel (0,0) of page 0. And the second byte contains the following pixel (1,0) of page 0. Again, this is exacly want you would expect, so nothing strange so far.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("==> So except for the color-depth, you could say screen 7 and 8 use the VRAM the same way.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Now for screen 5 and 6, it's a little different:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Screen 5:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When in screen 5 the first byte of VRAM contains the first two pixels (0,0) and (1,0) of page 0.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The second byte contains the first two pixels (0,0) and (1,0) of PAGE 2 !!!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Screen 6:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When in screen 6 the first byte of VRAM contains the first four pixels (0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(3,0) of page 0. The second byte contains the first four pixels of PAGE 2 !!!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("====> The graphic pages or stored as follows:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Absolute")
  HardwareHelp_L("address:       SCREEN 5    SCREEN 6    SCREEN 7    SCREEN 8")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("$0000-$7FFF    PAGE 0+2    PAGE 0+2    PAGE 0      PAGE 0")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("$8000-$FFFF    PAGE 1+3    PAGE 1+3    PAGE 1      PAGE 1")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("$0 in VRAM (*)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("                    PAGE 0                             PAGE 2")
  HardwareHelp_L("                 +---------+                       +---------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN_5:      (0,0)     (1,0)                   (0,0)     (1,0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN_6:   (0,0)(1,0) (2,0)(3,0)             (0,0)(1,0) (2,0)(3,0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("            +----+----+----+----+             +----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("            |      BYTE #0      |             |      BYTE #1      |")
  HardwareHelp_L("            +----+----+----+----+             +----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN_7:      (0,0)     (1,0)                   (2,0)     (3,0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN_8:           (0,0)                             (1,0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                      +------------ PAGE 0 -------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("$8000 in VRAM")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("                    PAGE 1                             PAGE 3")
  HardwareHelp_L("                 +---------+                       +---------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN_5:      (0,0)     (1,0)                   (0,0)     (1,0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN_6:   (0,0)(1,0) (2,0)(3,0)             (0,0)(1,0) (2,0)(3,0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("            +----+----+----+----+             +----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("            |      BYTE #0      |             |      BYTE #1      |")
  HardwareHelp_L("            +----+----+----+----+             +----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN_7:      (0,0)     (1,0)                   (2,0)     (3,0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN_8:           (0,0)                             (1,0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                      +------------ PAGE 1 -------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("(*) NOTE: the dollar sign in front of a number indicates a hexadecimal number.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The information provided above was discovered and documented by Jan Wilmans.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Additional notes by Lex Lechz: the behaviour described above is the one that can be noticed by trying to find out the VRAM-layout in the documented manner. After implementing this layout for the 1st time subsequent tests (with " + Chr(34) + "F-nano2" + Chr(34) + " and " + Chr(34) + "Space Manbow" + Chr(34) + ") showed that this description is not 100% correct. Additional facts are:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The described layout for SCREEN 5 and SCREEN 6 seems also to be valid for SCREEN 0 (40 and 80), SCREEN 1, SCREEN 2, SCREEN 3 and SCREEN 4. So only SCREEN 7, SCREEN 8 and other 64KB modes are exceptions from this rule.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("So we could say that basic modes (SCREEN 0 till SCREEN 6) are using " + Chr(34) + "flat" + Chr(34) + " linear adresses and higher modes have the " + Chr(34) + "tiled" + Chr(34) + " adress-space...")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Also: invalid display modes are compatible to the MSX1 (SCREEN 0-3) VRAM layout.")
  HardwareHelp_L("This has e.g. impact to the MSX1 game " + Chr(34) + "Hunchback" + Chr(34) + ", which temporarily disables the")
  HardwareHelp_L("display by (mistakenly?) selecting an invalid mode (while filling data in VRAM).")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Layout da VRAM - VRAM-Layout", "MSX-Video (V9958)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 videochip is implemented in the GFX9000 video-board.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The Videochip of the future (Sunrise Foundation)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The following sections a containing descriptions of all V9990 registers, both the ordinary  known and some 'secret' ones which I found during my work with the GFX9000 PowerBasic, Paint9000, Xbasic and more.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("(c) Henrik Gilvad 1996. May be used as long as it's for free!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("V9990 commands")
  HardwareHelp_L("V9990 ports")
  HardwareHelp_L("V9990 registers")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("V9990", "V9990 (VideoChip do futuro)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("COMMAND")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("11.1 Command execution method")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("After setting the  necessary parameter register, set the command to the operation code register, and it will be executed. As soon as execution is started, the status CE is set to " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + " and upon completion, it is reset to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + ". Furthermore, the interrupt flag CE is set to " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + ".")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Most commands are issued after the necessary registers out of the following parameters are set. Also some commands require output and input of the necessary data at and through the command data port after they are issued.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1. Source (for transfer) coordinate.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  2. Destination (for transfer) coordinate.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  3. Transfer range coordinate.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  4. Argument, logical operation and write mask.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  5. Font color")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("11.2 Parameter setting.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Source (for transfer) coordinate or address")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6     b5     b4     b3     b2     b1     b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#32 | SX7  | SX6  | SX5  | SX4  | SX3  | SX2  | SX1  | SX0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | SA7  | SA6  | SA5  | SA4  | SA3  | SA2  | SA1  | SA0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | KA7  | KA6  | KA5  | KA4  | KA3  | KA2  | KA1  | KA0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     -------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#33 | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0    |  0  | SX10 | SX9  | SX8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     -------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#34 | SY7  | SY6  | SY5  | SY4  | SY3  | SY2  | SY1  | SY0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | SA15 | SA14 | SA13 | SA12 | SA11 | SA10 | SA9  | SA8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | KA15 | KA14 | KA13 | KA12 | KA11 | KA10 | KA9  | KA8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     -------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#35 | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   | SY11 | SY10 | SY9  | SY8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   | SA18 | SA17 | SA16 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   | KA17 | KA16 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("There are 3 ways of setting depending on types of command as follows.")
  HardwareHelp_L("1) LMCM, LMMM, BMLX, SRCH, POINT")
  HardwareHelp_L("   SX0-10: Specified with the X coordinate of the image coordinate space (by")
  HardwareHelp_L("           dots) and rolling to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs when a larger value than the image")
  HardwareHelp_L("           size is used for setting. (When in P1 mode, screen " + Chr(34) + "A" + Chr(34) + " is selected")
  HardwareHelp_L("           at SX9=0 and screen " + Chr(34) + "B" + Chr(34) + " at SX9=1.)")
  HardwareHelp_L("   SY0-11: Specified with the Y coordinate of the image coordinate space (by")
  HardwareHelp_L("           dots) and rolling to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs when a larger value than the image")
  HardwareHelp_L("           size is used for setting.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2) CMMM, BMXL, BMLL")
  HardwareHelp_L("   SA0-18: Specified with the address on the VRAM map (by bytes).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("3) CMMK")
  HardwareHelp_L("   KA0-17: Specified with the kanji ROM address (by bytes).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Destination (for transfer) coordinate or address")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6     b5     b4     b3     b2     b1     b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#36 | DX7  | DX6  | DX5  | DX4  | DX3  | DX2  | DX1  | DX0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | DA7  | DA6  | DA5  | DA4  | DA3  | DA2  | DA1  | DA0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#37 | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   | DX10 | DX9  | DX8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#38 | DY7  | DY6  | DY5  | DY4  | DY3  | DY2  | DY1  | DY0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | DA15 | DA14 | DA13 | DA12 | DA11 | DA10 | DA9  | DA8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#39 | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   | DY11 | DY10 | DY9  | DY8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   | DA18 | DA17 | DA16 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("There are 2 ways of setting depending on types of command as follows.")
  HardwareHelp_L("1) LMMC, LMMV, LMMM, CMMC, CMMK, CMMM, BMXL, LINE, PSET, ADVN")
  HardwareHelp_L("   DX0-10: Specified with the X coordinate of the image coordinate space (by")
  HardwareHelp_L("           dots) and rolling to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs when a larger value than the image")
  HardwareHelp_L("           size is used for setting (When in P1 mode, screen " + Chr(34) + "A" + Chr(34) + " is selected")
  HardwareHelp_L("           at DX9=0 and screen " + Chr(34) + "B" + Chr(34) + " at DX9=1.)")
  HardwareHelp_L("   DY0-11: Specified with the Y coordinate of the image coordinate space (by")
  HardwareHelp_L("           dots) and rolling to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs when a larger value than the image")
  HardwareHelp_L("           size is used for setting.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2) BMLX, BMLL")
  HardwareHelp_L("   DA0-18: Specified with the address on the VRAM map (by bytes)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Transfer range coordinate or address")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6     b5     b4     b3     b2     b1     b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#40 | NX7  | NX6  | NX5  | NX4  | NX3  | NX2  | NX1  | NX0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | NA7  | NA6  | NA5  | NA4  | NA3  | NA2  | NA1  | NA0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | MJ7  | MJ6  | MJ5  | MJ4  | MJ3  | MJ2  | MJ1  | MJ0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#41 | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   | NX10 | NX9  | NX8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   | MJ11 | MJ10 | MJ9  | MJ8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#42 | NY7  | NY6  | NY5  | NY4  | NY3  | NY2  | NY1  | NY0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | NA15 | NA14 | NA13 | NA12 | NA11 | NA10 | NA9  | NA8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | MI7  | MI6  | MI5  | MI4  | MI3  | MI2  | MI1  | MI0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#43 | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   | NY11 | NY10 | NY9  | NY8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   | NA18 | NA17 | NA16 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   | MI11 | MI10 | MI9  | MI8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("There are 3 ways of setting depending on types of command as follows.")
  HardwareHelp_L("1) LMMC, LMMV, LMCM, LMMM, CMMC, CMMK, CMMM, BMXL, BMLX")
  HardwareHelp_L("   NX0-10: The number of dots in X direction of the image coordinate space is")
  HardwareHelp_L("           specified and rolling to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs when the specified range")
  HardwareHelp_L("           exceeds the image size. (Maximum values is 2048 dots when all " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + ".)")
  HardwareHelp_L("   NY0-11: The number of dots in Y direction of the image coordinate space is")
  HardwareHelp_L("           specified and rolling to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs when the specified range")
  HardwareHelp_L("           exceeds the image size. (Maximum value is 4096 when all " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + ".)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2) BMLL")
  HardwareHelp_L("   NA0-18: The number of bytes on the VRAM map is specified and rolling to")
  HardwareHelp_L("           address " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs when the specified range exceeds the VRAM")
  HardwareHelp_L("           capacity. (Maximum value is 512k bytes when all " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + ".)")
  HardwareHelp_L("           Note) 256 bytes when NA7 to NA0 are all " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34))
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("3) LINE")
  HardwareHelp_L("   MJ0-11: The length of the longer side of the rectangle whose diagonal line")
  HardwareHelp_L("           is the same as the drawn line is specified by dots. When the")
  HardwareHelp_L("           specified range exceeds the image size, rolling to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   MI0-11: The length of the shorter side of the rectangle whose line is the")
  HardwareHelp_L("           same as the drawn line is specified by dots. When the specified")
  HardwareHelp_L("           range exceeds the image size, rolling to " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " occurs.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Argument")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6     b5     b4       b3      b2     b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#44 | 0    |  0   |  0   |  0   | DIY  | DIX  | NEQ  | MAJ  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DIY DIX: The transfer direction (plus direction at " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " and minus direction at")
  HardwareHelp_L("         " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + ") from the base coordinates is specified. With BMXL and BMLX,")
  HardwareHelp_L("         however, the linear address specified side is fixed to " + Chr(34) + "plus" + Chr(34) + " and")
  HardwareHelp_L("         with BMLL, both X and Y are specified to the same direction.")
  HardwareHelp_L("NEQ:     In the border color specification for SRCH, " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " is for specified")
  HardwareHelp_L("         color detection and " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + " is for non-specified color detection.")
  HardwareHelp_L("MAJ:     In the line inclinatino specification for LINE, X is the longer side")
  HardwareHelp_L("         and Y the shorter side at " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " while Y is the longer side and X the")
  HardwareHelp_L("         shorter side at " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + ".")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Logical operation")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6     b5     b4       b3      b2     b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#45 | 0    |  0   |  0   | TP   | L11  | L10  | L01  | L00  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When  drawing  by means  of commands,  it is possible to write the value (WC) obtained  through the  logical operation using the value to be drawn (SC) and the targer value (DC). The content of the operation is specified as follows.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  L00: WC value for the bit with SC=0 and DC=0")
  HardwareHelp_L("  L01: WC value for the bit with SC=0 and DC=1")
  HardwareHelp_L("  L10: WC value for the bit with SC=1 and DC=0")
  HardwareHelp_L("  L11: WC value for the bit with SC=1 and DC=1")
  HardwareHelp_L("  TP : When this bit is " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + ", the data with SC as all " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " (by dot for the XY")
  HardwareHelp_L("       coordinates and by byte for the linear address) is not transferred.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("<Example>")
  HardwareHelp_L("        ------------------+---+---+---+---+-----+--+")
  HardwareHelp_L("        |Logical operation|L11|L10|L01|L00|Name |# |")
  HardwareHelp_L("        +-----------------+---+---+---+---+-----+--+")
  HardwareHelp_L("        |WC = SC          | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 | PSET|12|")
  HardwareHelp_L("        +-----------------+---+---+---+---+-----+--+")
  HardwareHelp_L("        |WC = !SC         | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 | NOT |3 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("        +-----------------+---+---+---+---+-----+--+")
  HardwareHelp_L("        |WC = (SC AND DC) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | AND |8 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("        +-----------------+---+---+---+---+-----+--+")
  HardwareHelp_L("        |WC = (SC OR DC)  | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | OR  |14|")
  HardwareHelp_L("        +-----------------+---+---+---+---+-----+--+")
  HardwareHelp_L("        |WC = (SC XOR DC) | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | XOR |6 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("        +-----------------+---+---+---+---+-----+--+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Write mask")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6     b5     b4     b3     b2     b1     b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#46 | WM7  | WM6  | WM5  | WM4  | WM3  | WM2  | WM1  | WM0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#47 | WM15 | WM14 | WM13 | WM12 | WM11 | WM10 | WM9  | WM8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When drawing  by means    of commands,  writing can be prohibited by bits. With WM0-7 and WM8-15, VRAM0 bits and VRAM1 bits are specified respectively. Then " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + " is for write enable and " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " is for write prohibit.")
  HardwareHelp_L("In P1  mode, writing  is prohibited on the side not specified as the transfer destination. (Screen " + Chr(34) + "A" + Chr(34) + ":R#46, Screen " + Chr(34) + "B" + Chr(34) + ":R#47)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Font color")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6     b5     b4     b3     b2     b1     b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#48 | FC7  | FC6  | FC5  | FC4  | FC3  | FC2  | FC1  | FC0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#49 | FC15 | FC14 | FC13 | FC12 | FC11 | FC10 | FC9  | FC8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#50 | BC7  | BC6  | BC5  | BC4  | BC3  | BC2  | BC1  | BC0  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#51 | BC15 | BC14 | BC13 | BC12 | BC11 | BC10 | BC9  | BC8  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The font color (color code) is specified for CMMC, CMMK and CMMM. Also, the drawing color  for LMMV, LINE and PSET and the border color for SRCH are specified by using FC0-15.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Correspondance with VRAM bit position is the same as write mask.")
  HardwareHelp_L(" FC0-15: Color code of font data " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34))
  HardwareHelp_L(" BC0-15: Color code of font data " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34))
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Note)  Depending on  conditions of R#6 (CLRM1, CLRM0), use the following data setting for FC0-15 of R#48 and R#49 (BC0-15 of R#50 and R#51).")
  HardwareHelp_L("  16 bit/dot --- Write 16 bit data for FC0-FC15")
  HardwareHelp_L("   8 bit/dot --- Write the same data for FC0-7 and FC8-15")
  HardwareHelp_L("   4 bit/dot --- Write the same data for FRC0-3, FC4-7, FC8-11 and FC12-15.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   2 bit/dot --- Fill FC0-15 with the same eight 2-bit unit data.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("11.3 Setting of operation code")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Operation code")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6     b5     b4       b3      b2     b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#52 | OP3  | OP2  | OP1  | OP0  | AYM  | AYE  | AXM  | AXE  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When a code from the list below is written in this register, the corresponding command will be executed.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("|OP3 OP2 OP1 OP0| Name | Operation                                          |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 0   0   0   0 | STOP |Command being executed is stopped.                  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 0   0   0   1 | LMMC |Data is transferred from CPU to VRAM rectangle area.|")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 0   0   1   0 | LMMV |VRAM rectangle area is painted out with FC0-15      |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 0   0   1   1 | LMCM |VRAM rectangle area data is transferred to CPU.     |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 0   1   0   0 | LMMM |Rectangle area data is transferred from VRAM to VRAM|")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 0   1   0   1 | CMMC |CPU character data is colordeveloped and transferred|")
  HardwareHelp_L("|               |      |to VRAM rectangle area.                             |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 0   1   1   0 | CMMK |Kanji ROM data is colordeveloped and transferred to |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|               |      |VRAM rectangle area.                                |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 0   1   1   1 | CMMM |VRAM character data is colordeveloped and transfer- |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|               |      |red to VRAM rectangle area.                         |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 1   0   0   0 | BMXL | Data on VRAM linear address is transferred to VRAM |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|               |      | rectangle area.                                    |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 1   0   0   1 | BMLX | VRAM rectangle area data is transferred onto VRAM  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|               |      | linear address.                                    |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 1   0   1   0 | BMLL | Data on VRAM linear address is transferred onto    |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|               |      | VRAM linear address.                               |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 1   0   1   1 | LINE | Straight line is drawn on XY coordinates.          |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 1   1   0   0 | SRCH | Search horizontally for Equal or Nonequal color    |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 1   1   0   1 | POINT| Color code of specified point is read out.         |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 1   1   1   0 | PSET | Drawing is executed at drawing point on X-Y coor.  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| 1   1   1   1 | ADVN | Drawing point on X-Y coordinates is shifted        |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+---------------+------+----------------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("With PSET and ADVN, the drawing point can be shifted after executing the command.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  AXE AXM")
  HardwareHelp_L("   0   0    (DX,DY) is used as a pointer.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   0   1    The pointer is not shifted.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1   0    The pointer is shifted to the right by 1 dot.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1   1    The pointer is shifted to the left by 1 dot.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("  AYE AYM")
  HardwareHelp_L("   0   0    The pointer is not shifted.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   0   1    The pointer is not shifted.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1   0    The pointer is shifted down by 1 dot.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1   1    The pointer is shifted up by 1 dot.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("11.4 Command data")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Comandos - V9990 commands", "V9990 (VideoChip do futuro)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("                       - THE VIDEOCHIP OF THE FUTURE -")
  HardwareHelp_L(" ")
  HardwareHelp_L("                                  V 9 9 9 0")
  HardwareHelp_L("                                 ===========")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("There have been no real new hardware developments for the MSX since the MSX turbo R. But now the MSX-Hndlergemein- schaft from Switzerland are working on the " + Chr(34) + "Graphics 9000" + Chr(34) + ", a 16-bit videocard for the MSX turbo R with the new V9990 by Yamaha. The " + Chr(34) + "Graphics 9000" + Chr(34) + " will be available in October or November 1994 for approximately US$ 290.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("EVALUATION BOARD")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Since the International MSX Fair in Tilburg on March 26th 1994 I'm the proud owner of one of the 10 V9990 evaluation boards which are in Europe at the moment. The evaluation board is a print (appr. 6" + Chr(34) + " x 6" + Chr(34) + ") with a lot of electronic stuff I don't understand with in the middle the V9990 itself which is square like the R800. The V9938 (MSX2 videochip) and V9958 (MSX2+) were rectangular. The connections on the board include a connect for an interface to the MSX turbo R and one for a scart lead.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("You can connect the board to a monitor or TV directly with a scart lead, so the image of the V9990 goes directly to the monitor. This is a small disadvantage if you have only one monitor like me, because this means you have to switch the leads when you want to work on the V9990 after working on the V9958 or vice versa. This problem will probably be solved with the Graphics 9000.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("You can connect a VGA monitor (640x400 or 640x480 in 16 colours) or even an LCD to the V9990, but unfortunately I haven't been able to test this yet.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("V9990 BASIC")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If you connect the V9990 to your turbo R and monitor and you switch the computer on, the screen will remain blank at first. This is because the operating system of the turbo R still uses the V9958. You have to execute a V9990 program before you can see anything.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Henrik Gilvad from Denmark modified the BASIC of the turbo R so it works with the V9990. He also modified the KUN compiler (also known as XBASIC). The V9990 BASIC, BIOS and KUN are loaded into the DRAM (*) of the turbo R. The BASIC is not completely compatible with the normal BASIC, it doesn't have to be and it's not possible due to some hardware differences. But there aren't so many differences so it's not hard at all to program something in the V9990 (KUN)BASIC.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("(*) Note: Because ROM is significantly slower then RAM on the turbo R, it has the possibility to copy the ROMs to the upper 64 kB of the main RAM which is called DRAM. The engine of the turbo R (S1990) makes it possible to use this DRAM as " + Chr(34) + "ROM" + Chr(34) + ".")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("An important difference between the V9938 and the V9990 is that the V9990 hasn't got a textmode. Henrik solved this problem by programming his own textmode in the P2-mode of the V9990. It's a very fast 64-column textmode with International character set, so the Japanese characters of the turbo R can't bother you anymore.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 BASIC supports the following screen modes:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("screen  remark")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("0       64 column textmode")
  HardwareHelp_L("1       will be 32 column textmode")
  HardwareHelp_L("2       P1 mode (256 x 212 dualplane patternmode)")
  HardwareHelp_L("3       P2 mode (512 x 212 patternmode)")
  HardwareHelp_L("4       identical to SCREEN 2")
  HardwareHelp_L("5..12   identical to MSX2/2+ except the sprites, only 2 sprites in")
  HardwareHelp_L("        one colour are possible")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 supports a lot more screen modes, which can easily be selected by a few VDP commands. For example, you can select the overscan mode by OUT &H67,1. ")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In the overscan mode you can use the entire screen, including the borders! The resolution is 384x240 in stead of 256x212 or 768x240 in stead of 512x212.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The following graphical instructions are supported by the V9990 BASIC:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("BLOAD,S, BSAVE,S, CIRCLE, COLOR, COPY, DRAW, KEY ON/OFF, LINE, LOCATE, PAINT, POINT, PRESET, PSET, PRINT, PRINT USING, PRINT#, PUT SPRITE, SCREEN, SET ADJUST, SET PAGE, SET SCROLL, VDP, VPEEK, VPOKE and WIDTH. Of course the non-graphical instructions are not modified.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("512 kB VRAM")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 has a lot of VRAM: 512 kB!!! This means that you have 16 pages in SCREEN 5 and 6 and 8 pages in SCREEN 7..12. Doesn't something like SET PAGE 13,15 look great?!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SPEEDY GONZALES")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 is extremely fast!!! For example, you can make a full screen smooth scroll in SCREEN 5 with a BASIC program using COPY! The copy command of the V9990 is 9 times faster than the " + Chr(34) + "high-speed" + Chr(34) + " copy command of the V9938! On the V9938 you have to use a " + Chr(34) + "logical" + Chr(34) + " copy if you want to copy pixels in stead of bytes or if you want to use logical operations. On the V9990 this isn't necessary so those copies are 23 times faster!!! Note: In MSX-BASIC all copies are treated as logical copies on the MSX2/2+/turbo R, so the COPY command in BASIC is 23 times faster with the V9990 BASIC!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I/O SPEED")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 has faster commands but it also allows faster I/O! The V9938 can hardly keep up with the Z80, so the R800 of the turbo R is slowed down when it communicates with the V9958. This isn't necessary with the V9990, so you can really use the speed of the R800.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The evaluation board and Graphics 9000 use I/O addresses &H60..&H6F. These ports are called P#0..P#F. P#0..P#7 are used for normal VDP operations, P#8..P#B are for the kanji ROMs (not supported on Graphics 9000, but the turbo R has its own kanji ROMs) and P#C..P#F are reserved for future use.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("FLI-PLAYER")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("On the International MSX Fair in Tilburg on April 3rd 1993 Anarchy from Germany/Switzerland released their Ray Tracing demo. This demo has a lot of beautiful ray-traced animations in SCREEN 8, which are real-time decrunched. It looks nice but it's not smooth because the V9938 is too slow.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Mi-Chi (a member of Anarchy) made a FLI-Player for the V9990. It works the same, some of the animations were used in the Ray Tracing demo too. But on the V9990 the animations are really SMOOTH!!! A fast harddisk (with SCSI interface from Henrik Gilvad) is recommended because some of the .FLI files are 400 kB and there is even a new .FLI player with files upto 2 MB. Unfortunately I can't watch these large files because I haven't got enough RAM.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DRAWING PROGRAMS")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The Graphics 9000 will be sold with two drawing programs. One for B1-mode, 256x212 with 64 colours out of a palette of 32768, and one for P2-mode, 512x212 with 16 colours out of a palette of 32768 colours. The B1-program looks like DD-Graph and the P2-program will have a lot of options and used pull down menus.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("VIDEO")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The Graphics 9000 will be capable of super imposing, which makes it ideal for videofreaks. Mi-Chi is working on a program for animations, wipes, etc. The advantages of the Graphics 9000 compared to the Philips 8280 are:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("- the V9990 is much faster")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("- on the 8280 you can't use the border, the V9990 has the overscan mode")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("- the V9990 has better graphics (upto 512x424 with 32768 colours) for digis")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Digitizing is not possible yet with the V9990, but it will be possible and that will be amazing because you can digitize in the 512x424 mode with 32768 colours!!! This means that you can't see the difference between the digi and the original!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("One of the demodisk are two 512x424 YUV digis. These digis are really brilliant, you can't see the difference with normal TV quality! YUV is a system for encoding 19268 colours using only 8 bits per pixel similar to the YJK system used in SCREEN 12 on the MSX2. It has the same disadvantage as YJK (colour spill because 4 adjacent pixels have the same U and V values, only the brightness (Y) can be different for each pixel), but you can hardly see it in such a high resolution. You can imagine the quality of pictures with 32768 colours without colourspill!!!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("GAMES")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I'm the programmer of UMAX, famous from our games Pumpkin Adventure and The Witch's Revenge. It's very frustrating if you can't program what you want because the possibilities and/or speed of the videochip. This happens a lot if you program something for the MSX2! The number of characters moving on the screen and the size of them is a problem, and a nice scrolling routine is even worse.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 has two modes ideal for games: P1 and P2. These are patternmodes, the screen consists of patterns of 8x8 pixels each. The resolutions are 256x212 and 512x212. With these pattern modes it is much more easy to make much nicer games for the V9990 than for the MSX2!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DUALPLANE")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The P1-mode is better known as the dualplane mode, because it has two screens which can scroll independently! You see the back screen where the front screen is transparent. It's very easy to make fantastic multi layer scrolls using this dualplane mode! Each plane can have one of the 4 palettes of 16 colours out of 32768.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("A very special feature of the P1-mode is a kind of hardware screensplit. You can select a kind " + Chr(34) + "border" + Chr(34) + " in 64-pixel units. Above and on the left-hand side of this border plane A is the front plane, and below and on the right-hand side of this border plane B is the front plane.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PATTERNS")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Because the V9990 has so much VRAM it can store over 15000 patterns. The format of the patterns is very nice, it's equal to a SCREEN 5 picture with 8x8 blocks. So it's very easy to use the patterns!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Games for the MSX2 are often in SCREEN 5 and use 8x8 blocks. The screen construction routine for such games is rather complicated, because for each pattern you have to calculate at which coordinates it is in the VRAM, and it has to be copied to the right position on the visible screen. This is slow and difficult.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("On the V9990 it's both very simple and fast: just VPOKE the pattern number (16 bits) in the pattern nametable. Are you now beginning to understand what I mean with " + Chr(34) + "it is much more easy to make much nicer games for the V9990 than for the MSX2" + Chr(34) + "? But this is not all!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SPRITES")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In P1 and P2 modes you can use upto 125 (one hundred and twenty five!) sprites, which is almost 4 times as much as the 32 sprites of the V9938. Upto 16 sprites can be displayed on a single horizontal line, so the times of blinking sprites are over!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("But the main advantage of the sprites is that, just like the patterns, they are SCREEN 5 graphics! So you can draw 16x16 sprites in DD-Graph and just load that into the VRAM. Without any conversion you can use those 16x16 blocks as sprites.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("On the MSX2 each horizontal line of a sprite can have only 1 colour, on the V9990 all colours out of one of the four palettes of 16 colours out of 32768 can be used. It is possible to get multi-coloured sprites on the MSX2, but you have to use 2 sprites for one " + Chr(34) + "object" + Chr(34) + " to get 3 colours on a horizontal line so you can only have 16 of those 3-colour sprites!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If you want the sprite to move behind a fence and still see the part of the sprite not covered by the fence, that's very hard to do on the MSX2. But all you need to do on the V9990 is putting the fence at the front plane and setting one bit of the sprite attributes!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Another advantage of the V9990 is that in the 512x212 P2-mode the spritepixels have the same size as screenpixels. On the MSX2 one sprite pixel is two screen pixels wide, and the x-coordinate can only be 0..255, on the V9990 the x- coordinate can be 0..511.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The B-modes haven't got sprites because they were not designed to use for games. They do have two special 32x32 cursor sprites.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SOFTWARE")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 is ideal for games, business applications and video applications. Mountainsoft from Switzerland are working on a game, there will be a V9990 version of the DTP program Aladin and a lot more software (including the drawing and video programs I already mentioned) are being developed.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Henrik made a program to watch pictures from a Photo CD using a CD-ROM drive and V9990 and Mi-Chi made a very professional viewer for a lot of formats including GIF, BMP and PCX. The GIF, BMP and PCX pictures look a lot better on the V9990 than on the MSX2 because the V9990 can display more colours (32768) at a high resolution (512x424).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("A lot of groups, including UMAX, have plans to make a game for the V9990.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("INTERRUPTS")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("These paragraph is interesting for programmers, if you don't know anything about interrupts you'd better skip it.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 can generate interrupts caused by the following events:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1) Vertical blank (50/60 Hz)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2) When displaying a certain line (line interrupt)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("3) When a command is finished")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4) When displaying at a certain HORIZONTAL position")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("5) At the start of displaying EACH line")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9938 only supports 1 and 2. The advantage of 3 is clear. With 4 you can make a " + Chr(34) + "vertical screensplit" + Chr(34) + ", which is not possible on the V9938 (not a stable one) and 5 is very nice for " + Chr(34) + "waves" + Chr(34) + ".")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To see what caused the interrupt you have to read a status register on the MSX2, on the V9990 you only have to read the interrupt flag port (P#6) which is much faster.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PROGRAMMING")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Programming for the V9990 is more simple or at least as simple as programming for the V9958. A lot of things take less IN/OUT instructions and so they take less time and less bytes.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I already mentioned the interrupt flag port, the V9990 also has a status port with a lot of nice things like " + Chr(34) + "vertical non-display period" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "horizontal non-display period" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "command being executed" + Chr(34) + ", etc. Reading from/writing to the VRAM is more simple too, just compare the following routines to see what I mean!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("; SetWriteV9938")
  HardwareHelp_L("; In: CHL = VRAM address (17 bits)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" ")
  HardwareHelp_L("        LD      A,H")
  HardwareHelp_L("        AND     #3F")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OR      #40")
  HardwareHelp_L("        EX      AF,AF'")
  HardwareHelp_L("        LD      A,H")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OR      C")
  HardwareHelp_L("        RLCA")
  HardwareHelp_L("        RLCA")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OUT     (#99),A")
  HardwareHelp_L("        LD      A,128+14")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OUT     (#99),A")
  HardwareHelp_L("        LD      A,L")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OUT     (#99),A")
  HardwareHelp_L("        EX      AF,AF'")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OUT     (#99),A")
  HardwareHelp_L("        RET")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("; SetWriteV9990")
  HardwareHelp_L("; In: EHL = VRAM address (19 bits)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" ")
  HardwareHelp_L("        LD      C,#64")
  HardwareHelp_L("        XOR     A")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OUT     (C),A")
  HardwareHelp_L("        DEC     C")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OUT     (C),L")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OUT     (C),H")
  HardwareHelp_L("        OUT     (C),E")
  HardwareHelp_L("        RET")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("RESOLUTIONS AND COLOURS")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9990 supports the following resolutions:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("mode   resolution     remark")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("P1     256x212(424)   dualplane patternmode")
  HardwareHelp_L("P2     512x212(424)   patternmode")
  HardwareHelp_L("B1     256x212(424)")
  HardwareHelp_L("B2     384x240(480)   overscan")
  HardwareHelp_L("B3     512x212(424)")
  HardwareHelp_L("B4     768x240(480)   overscan")
  HardwareHelp_L("B5     640x400")
  HardwareHelp_L("B6     640x480")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Interlaced vertical resolutions are shown in parentheses " + Chr(34) + "()" + Chr(34) + ". These resolutions can be combined with the palette systems below, so a lot of different types of screen modes are possible. Systems using 8 or 16 bits per pixel are not possible for B4..B6, P1 and P2 only work with 4 bits per pixel.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("bits/pixel  colours   system")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("16           32768    simultaneously (TrueColour)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 8              64    out of a palette of 32768")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 8             256    like SCREEN 8")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 8           19268    like SCREEN 12 (YJK)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 8           19268    YUV")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 4              16    out of a palette of 32768")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 2               4    out of a palette of 32768")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DISPLAY SIZE VS IMAGE SIZE")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("A very nice feature of the V9990 is the image size, which is much larger than the actual display size most of the time, so you can only see a part of the total " + Chr(34) + "image" + Chr(34) + " on your screen. Which part is determined by the scroll registers, so you can scroll smoothly in all directions in all screen modes.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The image size depends of the number of bits used per pixel. The following table is valid for the bitmap modes (B1..B6).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("bits/pixel      resolutions")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2               256x8192, 512x4096, 1024x2048, 2048x1024")
  HardwareHelp_L("4               256x4096, 512x2048, 1024x1024, 2048x 512")
  HardwareHelp_L("8               256x2048, 512x1024, 1024x 512, 2048x 256")
  HardwareHelp_L("16              256x1024, 512x 512, 1024x 256")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In the P1 mode there are two images of 64x64 patterns each (512x512 pixels), the P2 mode has one image of 128x64 patterns (1024x512).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If you use the B1 mode for a game you can put an entire level of the game into the VRAM, because there is space for 16 screens if you use 16 colours. You don't have to use blocks but you can just draw the entire level. Of course the P1 and P2 modes are better for games because they have sprites.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("INTERLACED")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The V9938 also had an interlaced possibility, but it wasn't used very often because the odd lines of the picture have to be in one page and the even lines in another. Because of this a picture has to be converted before it can be shown in interlaced mode. Because of this animations are almost impossible.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The interlaced mode of the V9990 is much nicer, because it just doubles the display size of the picture. No conversions are necessary and animations are just as easy as in a non interlaced mode.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("By the way, the V9990 has a undocumented mode with 16 colours and a horizontal resolution of 1024. Together with the interlaced possibility the maximum resolution a V9990 can show on a normal TV is 1024x424! I must admit the quality isn't very high on my TV, but this should improve if you use a better monitor.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("FINALLY")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I didn't cover all the possibilities of the V9990, but I think it is more than clear that this videochip offers revolutionary possibilities on an MSX.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("A lot of things that are not possible at all on a V9958 or only with a lot of trouble, can be programmed for the V9990 in just a few lines of BASIC! You can imagine what the possibilities are if you use machine code!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I'm going to start with a V9990 programming course in both BASIC and ML as soon as the Graphics 9000 is available.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("It's a pity the V9990 wasn't finished soon enough (autumn of 1991) to be used in the MSX turbo R, which was released in the autumn of 1990. Most MSX users have ancient hardware nowadays. The V9938 was developed in 1985, so it's almost ten years old. On other systems it's very normal to upgrade your system every few years.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This has been possible on the MSX (MSX1 1983, MSX2 1985, MSX2+ 1988, MSX turbo R 1990), and now it will be possible again to upgrade your system with a Graphics 9000 and a Moonsound (16-bit soundcard with OPL4). I hope that a lot of MSX users around the world will buy a Graphics 9000 and a Moonsound.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Of course there isn't so much software at first, and because of this people don't buy the Graphics 9000, there will be no more software for it, etc. We have to break this vicious circle! Just buy a Graphics 9000 (and a turbo R if you haven't got one yet), then the software will follow soon. I can assure you a lot of groups who develop software can't wait to start programming for the V9990!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("A lot of groups who are developing software for the MSX2 at the moment are not challenged anymore, they made a few games and they want to move on. This is not possible with the MSX2. If we don't upgrade our hardware they will start developing software for other systems like the PC. Unfortunately a lot of groups already made that decision. The only way to stop more groups to take this bad step is to support the new Graphics 9000 and Moonsound.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Stefan Boer")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("smboer@cs.vu.nl")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("(c) Sunrise Foundation 1994")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Sunrise Foundation")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("P.O. Box 178")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1530 AD  Wormer")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The Netherlands")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Futuro - V9990", "V9990 (VideoChip do futuro)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("V9990 I/O port specifications")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Port GFX9000 Description")
  HardwareHelp_L("---- ---------------------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#0  #60 VRAM DATA")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#1  #61 PALETTE DATA")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#2  #62 COMMAND DATA")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#3  #63 REGISTER DATA")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#4  #64 REGISTER SELECT")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#5  #65 STATUS")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#6  #66 INTERRUPT FLAG")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#7  #67 SYSTEM CONTROL")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#8  #68 Primary standard kanji ROM lower order address")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#9  #69 Primary standard kanji ROM higher order address")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#9  #69 Primary standard kanji ROM data")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#A  #6A Secondary standard kanji ROM lower order address")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#B  #6B Secondary standard kanji ROM higher order address")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#B  #6B Secondary standard kanji ROM data ")
  HardwareHelp_L("------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("VRAM DATA PORT (Read / Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#0  |VD7|VD6|VD5|VD4|VD3|VD2|VD1|VD0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The data written in this port is written in the VRAM address specified by using #0 to 3. If writing to this port has taken place before completion of data write in VRAM, a WAIT signal is output. By reading through this port, the VRAM address data specified by using R#3 to 5 is obtained. If reading has taken place at this port before data preparation, a WAIT signal is output. When VRAM read address has been modified to prepare the VRAM address data at completion of data write to R#5, it must be written in R#5.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PALETTE DATA PORT (Read / Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#1  |PD7|PD6|PD5|PD4|PD3|PD2|PD1|PD0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The data written in this port is written in the internal palette RAM. By reading through this port, the internal palette RAM data can be obtained. A WAIT signal is output till the data has been prepared. The palette RAM address is specified by using R#14. The data format is as follows.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("      +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("RED   |!YS| - | - | R4| R3| R2| R1| R0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("      +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("GREEN | - | - | - | G4| G3| G2| G1| G0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("      +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("BLUE  | - | - | - | B4| B3| B2| B1| B0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("      +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("COMMAND DATA PORT (Read / Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#2  |CD7|CD6|CD5|CD4|CD3|CD2|CD1|CD0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The command data is read and written through this port. Access to this port when the status TR bit is " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " while the command is being executed will result in an output of WAIT signal.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("REGISTER DATA PORT (Read / Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#3  |RD7|RD6|RD5|RD4|RD3|RD2|RD1|RD0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The data written in this port is written in the register specified by using P#4. By reading through this port, the register data specified by using P#4 can be obtained.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("REGISTER SELECT PORT (Write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#4  |WII|RII|RA5|RA4|RA3|RA2|RA1|RA0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Using RA5 to 0, the register No. to which access is made through P#3 is specified. The register No. undergoes a plus 1 increment through P#3 access. However, when WII is " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + ", increment in writing at P#3 is prohibited and when RII is " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + ", increment in reading at P#3 is prohibited.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("STATUS PORT (Read only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#5  |TR |VR |HR |BD | 0 |MCS|E0 |CE |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Various types of status data can be read out as follows. TR: Command data transfer ready. It is reset through P#2 access. VR: Vertical non-display period. HR: Horizontal non-display period. BD: Border color detect at completion of SRCH command (becomes " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + " when detected) EO: In the second field period during interlace. CE: Command being executed. MCS: Content of P#7")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("MCS INTERRUPT FLAG PORT (Read / Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#6  | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |CE |HI |VI |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The interrupt flags can be read out as follows. CE: Command completion flag HI: Display position flag VI: Vertical display period completion flag By writing " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + ", the related bit flag is reset.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SYSTEM CONTROL PORT (Write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#7  | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |SRS|MCS|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SRS:Writing " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34) + " will set all ports except this one in " + Chr(34) + "power ON reset" + Chr(34) + " state. " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + " should be written to cancel it. MCS:The internally used master clock is selected. 1:MCKIN terminal (14.318MHz Overscan on GFX9000) 0:XTAL1 terminal (21.4772MHz Normal on GFX9000) NOTE) XTAL1 terminal is selected when " + Chr(34) + "power ON reset" + Chr(34) + ". If specification is not made properly when either XTAL1 or MCKIN terminal is used, specifi- cations for external input clock timing will not be fulfilled and a chip will be damaged in the worst case. As a measure to prevent this, it is necessary to supply clock signals to both terminals. The above does not apply when both terminals are used.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Primary standard kanji ROM ADDRESS PORT (Write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5    b4    b3    b2    b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#8  | - | - |K1A10| K1A9| K1A8| K1A7| K1A6| K1A5|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#9  | - | - |K1A16|K1A15|K1A14|K1A13|K1A12|K1A11|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The font (16x16 bits) address for the primary standard kanji ROM which is read through P#9 is specified. It is necessary to write in the order of P#9 and then P#8 to prepare data for the kanji ROM address at completion of writing to P#8.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Primary standard kanji ROM DATA PORT (Read only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7   b6   b5   b4   b3   b2   b1   b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#9  |K1D7|K1D6|K1D5|K1D4|K1D3|K1D2|K1D1|K1D0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("It is possible to read the data of the font address specified by using P#8 and P#9 of the primary standard kanji ROM. A WAIT signal is output till the data has been prepared. By reading 32 times continuously, it is possible to obtain 32 bytes data in the order of the upper left, upper right, lower left and lower right blocks (of 8 bytes each) of the 16x16 bits font.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Secondary standard kanji ROM ADDRESS PORT (Write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5    b4    b3    b2    b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#A  | - | - |K2A10| K2A9| K2A8| K2A7| K2A6| K2A5|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#B  | - | - |K2A16|K2A15|K2A14|K2A13|K2A12|K2A11|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +---+---+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The font (16x16 bits) address for the secondary standard kanji ROM which is read through P#9 is specified. It is necessary to write in the order of P#B and then P#A to prepare data for the kanji ROM address at completion of writing to P#A.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Secondary standard kanji ROM DATA PORT (Read only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("     b7   b6   b5   b4   b3   b2   b1   b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("P#C |K2D7|K2D6|K2D5|K2D4|K2D3|K2D2|K2D1|K2D0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("It is possible to read the data of the font address specified by using P#A and P#B of the secondary standard kanji ROM. A WAIT signal is output till the data has been prepared. By reading 32 times continuously, it is possible to obtain 32 bytes data in the order of the upper left, upper right, lower left and lower right blocks (of 8 bytes each) of the 16x16 bits font. Note) GFX9000 does not contain a kanji ROM. GFX9000 I/O port #6F is used for Superimpose control.")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Portas - V9990 ports", "V9990 (VideoChip do futuro)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("This TED file contains a description of all V9990 registers, both the ordinary  known and  some 'secret' ones which I found during my work with the GFX9000 PowerBasic, Paint9000, Xbasic and more.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("(c) Henrik Gilvad 1996. May be used as long as it's for free!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("V9990   Register specifications")
  HardwareHelp_L("------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#0-2   VRAM WRITE ADDRESS                    (W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#3-5   VRAM READ ADDRESS                     (W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#6,7   SCREEN MODE                           (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#8     CONTROL                               (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#9-12  INTERRUPT                             (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#13    PALETTE CONTROL                       (W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#14    PALETTE POINTER                       (W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#15    BACK DROP COLOR                       (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#16    DISPLAY ADJUST                        (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#17-24 SCROLL CONTROL                        (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#25    SPRITE PATTERN GENERATOR BASE ADDRESS (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#26    LCD CONTROL                           (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#27    PRIORITY CONTROL                      (R/W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#28    SPRITE PALETTE CONTROL                (W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#32-52 COMMAND                               (W)")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#53-54 COMMAND                               (R)")
  HardwareHelp_L("------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("VRAM WRITE ADDRESS (Write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("===============================")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7     b6     b5     b4     b3     b2     b1     b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#0  |CVWA7 |CVWA6 |CVWA5 |CVWA4 |CVWA3 |CVWA2 |CVWA1 |CVWA0 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#1  |CVWA15|CVWA14|CVWA13|CVWA12|CVWA11|CVWA10|CVWA9 |CVWA8 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#2  |CVWAIH|  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   |CVWA18|CVWA17|CVWA16|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("CVWA0-18 : VRAM write address")
  HardwareHelp_L("CVWAIH =0: CVWA undergoes an increment of plus 1 only at the end of writing")
  HardwareHelp_L("           to P#0.")
  HardwareHelp_L("       =1: No increment.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("VRAM READ ADDRESS (Write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("==============================")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7     b6     b5     b4     b3     b2     b1     b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#3  |CVRA7 |CVRA6 |CVRA5 |CVRA4 |CVRA3 |CVRA2 |CVRA1 |CVRA0 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#4  |CVRA15|CVRA14|CVRA13|CVRA12|CVRA11|CVRA10|CVRA9 |CVRA8 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#5  |CVRAIH|  0   |  0   |  0   |  0   |CVRA18|CVRA17|CVRA16|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("CVRA0-18 : VRAM read address")
  HardwareHelp_L("CVRAIH =0: CVRA undergoes an increment of plus 1 only at the end of reading")
  HardwareHelp_L("           at P#0.")
  HardwareHelp_L("       =1: No increment.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN MODE (Read / Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("==========================")
  HardwareHelp_L("     b7    b6    b5    b4    b3    b2    b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#6 |DSPM1|DSPM0|DCKM1|DCKM0|XIMM1|XIMM0|CLRM1|CLRM0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#7 |  0  |C25M | SM1 | SM  | PAL | EO  | IL  | HSCN|")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("DSPM1-DSPM0:    Display mode selection")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     1       Stand-by mode (non-display, no VRAM refresh, kanji readable)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     0       Bit map mode (B1~B6)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     1       P2 mode")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     0       P1 mode")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DCKM1-DCKM0     Dot clock selection (usind in combination with MCS of P#7)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                MCS=0   DOTS    MCS=1   DOTS")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     0       XTAL1   1024    MCKIN    768")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     1       XTAL1/2  512    MCKIN/2  384")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     0       XTAL1/4  256    MCKIN/4  192")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("MCS=1 means that we are in the socalled 'Overscan' mode. This means that most of the 'border' area are also used for drawing pixel data. In the Y direction NTSC will  have 240  dots in  Overscan mode (480 in Interlace), PAL will have around 290 dots (580 in Interlace).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This gives us 'new' screenmodes: (not mentioned by Yamaha)")
  HardwareHelp_L("1024 x 212 (424) dots")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 768 x 290 (580) dots (PAL)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("XIMM1-XIMM0     Selection of number of dots in X direction of image space")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     1       2048 dots")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     0       1024 dots")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     1        512 dots")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     0        256 dots")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CLRM1-CLRM0     Selection of bit number per dot")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     1       16 bits/dot")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     0        8 bits/dot")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     1        4 bits/dot")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     0        2 bits/dot")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("C25M            Selection of 640x480 mode, valid when HSCN is " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34))
  HardwareHelp_L("  1             B6 mode (Requires VGA oscillator instead of 21.4772)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0             P1, P2, B1~B5 mode")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SM1             Selection of total number of vertical lines during non-")
  HardwareHelp_L("                interlace, NTSC")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1             263 lines (in combination with SM, the sub-carrier phase is")
  HardwareHelp_L("                inverted for each frame.)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0             262 lines")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SM              Selection of horizontal frequency (invalid when in B5 and B6)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1             1H = fsc/227.5 (the sub-carrier phase is inverted for each")
  HardwareHelp_L("                line.)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0             1H = fsc/228")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PAL             Selection of PAL mode (invalid when in B5 and B6 modes)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1             Vertical frequency becomes PAL specification.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0             Vertical frequency becomes NTSC specification.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("EO              Selection of vertical resolution during interlace.")
  HardwareHelp_L("                (invalid when in B5 and B6 modes)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1             Twice the vertical resolution during Non-interlace")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0             Same as during Non-interlace")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("IL              Selection of interlace mode (invalid when in B5 and B6 modes)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1             Interlace")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0             Non-interlace")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("HSCN            Selection of horizontal high scan mode (VGA)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1             B5, B6 modes")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0             P1, P2, B1~B4 modes")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CONTROL (Read/Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("====================")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +----+---+---+----+---+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#8 |DISP|SPD|YSE|VWTE|VWM|DMAE|VSL1|VSL0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +----+---+---+----+---+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("DISP    Screen display enable")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     Display appears on screen according to the VRAM content.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     Back drop color is displayed all over the screen.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SPD     Sprite (Cursor in B5~B6 modes) non-display")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     Sprite (cursor) is not displayed")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     Sprite (cursor) is displayed")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("YSE     /YS signal output enable (Super impose layer select)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     /YS signal is output.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     /YS signal is not output (/YS terminal constantly low)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("VWTE    VRAM serial data bus input/output control during digitization.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     Dummy write transfer is executed during horizontal retrace line")
  HardwareHelp_L("        interval.")
  HardwareHelp_L("        (The serial data bus of VRAM becomes an input terminal.)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     Read transfer is executed during horizontal retrace line interval.")
  HardwareHelp_L("        (The serial data bus of VRAM becomes an output terminal)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("VWM     VRAM write control during digitization")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1    Write transfer is executed during horizontal retrace line interval.")
  HardwareHelp_L("        (The data inputted into the serial data bus of VRAM during display")
  HardwareHelp_L("        period undergoes write transfer.)")
  HardwareHelp_L("   0    Write transfer is not executed.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DMAE    /DREQ signal output enable")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1    The signal is synchronized to the status TR while command is executed")
  HardwareHelp_L("        and output at /DREQ terminal.")
  HardwareHelp_L("   0    /DREQ terminal remains as high level.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("VSL1 VSL0       Selection of video memory configuration")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1    0        256k word x 4 bit VRAM, 4 units (512k byte)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0    1        128k word x 8 bit VRAM, 2 units (256k byte)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0    0         64k word x 4 bit VRAM, 4 units (128k byte)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Note)    Always use the 256k word x 4 bit VRAM on GFX9000 !")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("INTERRUPT (Read/Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("======================")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2   b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +----+---+---+---+---+----+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#9  | 0  | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |IECE|IEH|IEV|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +----+---+---+---+---+----+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#10 |IL7 |IL6|IL5|IL4|IL3| IL2|IL1|IL0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +----+---+---+---+---+----+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#11 |IEHM| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |  0 |IL9|IL8|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +----+---+---+---+---+----+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#12 | 0  | 0 | 0 | 0 |IX3| IX2|IX1|IX0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +----+---+---+---+---+----+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("IECE    Command end interrupt enable control")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     /INT0 terminal becomes low level when CE flag of P#6 is " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34))
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     /INT0 terminal does not change according to CE flag")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("IEV     Interrupt enable during vertical retrace line interval.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     /INT0 terminal becomes low level when VI flag of P#6 is " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34))
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     /INT0 terminal does not change according to VI flag")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("IEH     Display position interrupt enable (Interrupt position is specified")
  HardwareHelp_L("        with IL0-9, IX0-3 and IEHM.)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     /INT1 terminal becomes low level when HI flag of P#6 is " + Chr(34) + "1" + Chr(34))
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     /INT1 terminal does not change according to HI flag.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("IL0-9   Specification of vertical position where display position interrupt")
  HardwareHelp_L("        occurs (Specified by means of LINE no. with the display start as 0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("IX0-3   Specification of horizontal position where display position interrupt")
  HardwareHelp_L("        occurs (Specified  by unit  of 64 master clock with the display start")
  HardwareHelp_L("        as " + Chr(34) + "0" + Chr(34) + ")")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("IEHM    Selection of vertical position for display position interrupt")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1    Every line (IL0-9 are ignored.)")
  HardwareHelp_L("   0    Specified line")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PALETTE CONTROL (Write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("============================")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7    b6   b5   b4    b3    b2    b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+---+------+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#13|PLTM1|PLTM0|YAE|PLTAIH|PLT05|PLT04|PLT03|PLT02|")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+---+------+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("PLTM1 PLTM0     Selection of color palette mode")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     1       YUV mode (8 bit / dot)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     0       YJK mode (8 bit / dot)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     1       256 color mode (like Screen 8 on MSX)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     0       Palette mode (2, 4, 16 or 64 colors)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("YAE     Selection of YJK (YUV) and RGB mixing mode (valid when in YUV/YJK)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     YJK (YUV) and RGB images are displayed together (Like screen 10/11)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     Only YJK (YUV) image is displayed.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PLTAIH  Palette Number increment control at color palette read-out.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     R#14 palette pointer is not changed by P#1 read-out.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     After P#1 read-out. R#14 palette pointer undergoes an increment.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PLT05-2 Still Screen palette number offset.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PALETTE POINTER (Write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("============================")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7    b6    b5    b4    b3    b2    b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#14|PLTA5|PLTA4|PLTA3|PLTA2|PLTA1|PLTA0|PLTP1|PLTP0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("PLTA5-0         Palette number (color code)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PLTP1 PLTP0     Specification of RGB")
  HardwareHelp_L("  1     0       Blue")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     1       Green")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0     0       Red")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("After completion of access to P#1, an increment takes place by plus 1 only.")
  HardwareHelp_L("PLT1 and 0 are used as a 0 to 2 ternary counter.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("BACK DROP COLOR (Read/Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +---+---+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#15| 0 | 0 |BDC5|BDC4|BDC3|BDC2|BDC1|BDC0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +---+---+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("BDC5-0  Palette number of back drop color")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DISPLAY ADJUST (Read/Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("===========================")
  HardwareHelp_L("      b7  b6  b5  b4  b3  b2  b1  b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("R#16|ADJV3|ADJV2|ADJV1|ADJV0|ADJH3|ADJH2|ADJV1|ADJV0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("    +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("ADJV3-0 Vertical display position adjustment 8 9 ...15 0 1 ... 6 7")
  HardwareHelp_L("        (by line)                           Down    Standard     Up")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("ADJH3-0 Horizontal display position adjustment 8 9 ... 15 0 1 ... 6 7")
  HardwareHelp_L("                                             Right    Standard    Left")
  HardwareHelp_L("        P1 and B1 by 1 dot unit")
  HardwareHelp_L("        P2, B2 and B3 by 2 dot unit")
  HardwareHelp_L("        B4, B5 and B6 by 4 dot unit")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCROLL CONTROL (Read/Write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("===========================")
  HardwareHelp_L("       b7     b6    b5    b4     b3     b2     b1    b0")
  HardwareHelp_L("     +------+-----+-----+------+------+------+-----+-----+")
