  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("According to the MSX2 Hardware Specification, ports #40-#4F are forming the so-called")
  HardwareHelp_L("'switched I/O ports'. With these the limitation of a maximum number of 256 I/O ports")
  HardwareHelp_L("can be overcome, although it does require a little additional logic on the hardware's")
  HardwareHelp_L("behalf. The port numbers #41-#4F are the actual switched ports, and the device 'connected'")
  HardwareHelp_L("(e.g. listening to) to those ports is determined by the device ID written to port #40.")
  HardwareHelp_L("This device ID can be a number from 1-254, and when port #40 is read it returns the")
  HardwareHelp_L("complement of the current device ID.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Port range | Description")
  HardwareHelp_L("40 | Device ID register")
  HardwareHelp_L("41-4F | Switched I/O ports")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("ID numbers between 1 and 127 are manufacturer ID numbers, and ID numbers 128 to 254 are")
  HardwareHelp_L("device ID's. As a basic rule, those devices which are designed specifically for one")
  HardwareHelp_L("machine should contain the manufacturer's company ID, while periperhal devices which can")
  HardwareHelp_L("be used on all MSX computers should have a device ID number. Also, the Z80 actually has a")
  HardwareHelp_L("16-bit I/O addressing space so it is recommended to access in 16-bit by decoding the upper")
  HardwareHelp_L("8 bits for those ID's which might be expanded in the future (although I have personally only")
  HardwareHelp_L("seen this done in the ADVRAM hardware). Especially for devices which are connected with their")
  HardwareHelp_L("make ID it can be expected that they might eventually need more addressing space, and with this")
  HardwareHelp_L("it will be future-proofed.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("List of BIOS extension device ID's:")
  HardwareHelp_L("ID | Maker/Device")
  HardwareHelp_L("1 | ASCII/Microsoft")
  HardwareHelp_L("2 | Canon")
  HardwareHelp_L("3 | Casio")
  HardwareHelp_L("4 | Fujitsu")
  HardwareHelp_L("5 | Fujitsu")
  HardwareHelp_L("6 | Hitachi")
  HardwareHelp_L("7 | Kyocera")
  HardwareHelp_L("8 | Matsushita (Panasonic)")
  HardwareHelp_L("9 | Mitsubishi")
  HardwareHelp_L("10 | NEC")
  HardwareHelp_L("11 | Nippon Gakki")
  HardwareHelp_L("12 | JVC")
  HardwareHelp_L("13 | Philips")
  HardwareHelp_L("14 | Pioneer")
  HardwareHelp_L("15 | Sanyo")
  HardwareHelp_L("16 | Sharp")
  HardwareHelp_L("17 | SONY")
  HardwareHelp_L("18 | Spectravideo")
  HardwareHelp_L("19 | Toshiba")
  HardwareHelp_L("20 | Spectravideo")
  HardwareHelp_L("128 | Image Scanner (Matsushita)")
  HardwareHelp_L("247 | Lex Lechz: Required for Kanji modes 1 and 3 (on MSX2, MSX2+ and TR)")
  HardwareHelp_L("254 | MPS2 (ASCII)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Some example code, this enables the 6MHz mode on Panasonic FS-A1WX/WSX/FX computers:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Port | Panasonic extensions")
  HardwareHelp_L("40 | = 8 (Panasonic manufacturer ID)")
  HardwareHelp_L("41 | Available on FS-A1FX/WX/WSX only:")
  HardwareHelp_L("      bit 0: CPU speed mode (0=5.37MHz, 1=3.57MHz)")
  HardwareHelp_L("Available on FS-A1FX/WX/WSX/ST/GT only:")
  HardwareHelp_L("      bit 7: Firmware switch status (0=on, 1=off) (read only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("	LD	A,8")
  HardwareHelp_L("	OUT 	(040H),A	;out the manufacturer code 8 (Panasonic) to I/O port 40h")
  HardwareHelp_L("	IN	A,(040H)	;read the value you have just written")
  HardwareHelp_L("	CPL			;complement all bits of the value")
  HardwareHelp_L("	CP	8		;if it does not match the value you originally wrote,")
  HardwareHelp_L("	JR	NZ,Not_WX	;it is not a WX/WSX/FX.")
  HardwareHelp_L("	XOR	A		;write 0 to I/O port 41h")
  HardwareHelp_L("	OUT	(041H),A	;and the mode changes to high-speed clock")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("So you can check if certain extensions are available and ready to be accessed by reading")
  HardwareHelp_L("back the value you just wrote to the device ID register. If that value is not the complement")
  HardwareHelp_L("of what you wrote, then the extensions are not present. ")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Portas I/O comutadas - Switched I/O-ports", "I/O-Port map")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("Available since: MSX Turbo-R")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Because the timelength of the execution of commands is not uniform, it is not possible to make some sort of wait-timing with the execution time of commands. For making in possible to check timelength between 10 usec and 10 msec, a system timer has been build in. The systemtimer will be increased every 42 clockpulses of the system clock (10.738635 MHz), (10.738635MHz/42 = 255.68KHz) with a writeable 16bit cleardata timer.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The least significant bit will be increased every 3.911 usec. The time that elapse between the clearance of the counter until it becomes 1, is between 0 and 3.911 usec. When reading the counter, there is not latch function of the data. Please notice that when a clockpulse enters while you are reading the lower or upper byte of the counter, the correct value of the counter can not be read.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("       figure 1.6 I/O port of the systemtimer")
  HardwareHelp_L("  ----------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("  I/O port   R/W    function")
  HardwareHelp_L("  ----------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0E6H        W      writing any data will clear the counter")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0E6H        R      reading the lower 8 bits of the counter ")
  HardwareHelp_L("  0E7H        R      reading the higher 8 bits of the counter")
  HardwareHelp_L("  ----------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Clearing the counter may have some effect on programs that are called from and interrupt routine etc and that use this timer. A program could basically look like the program below:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("        countlow        equ     0E6H    ;lowbyte counter")
  HardwareHelp_L("        counthigh       equ     0E7H    ;highbyte counter")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("        ;The program will wait for the time designated with B")
  HardwareHelp_L("        ;The time that will be waited is B*3.911 usec.")
  HardwareHelp_L("        ;The time difference will be about -3.911 - +0 usec.")
  HardwareHelp_L("        ;It is not allowed to assign 0 to the B register.")
  HardwareHelp_L("        ;It is not allowed to generate interrupts while using this program.")
  HardwareHelp_L("        ;The contents of register C,A,F will be damaged.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("        wait:")
  HardwareHelp_L("                        in      a,(countlow)  ;get current counter value")
  HardwareHelp_L("                        ld      c,a           ;save this  ")
  HardwareHelp_L("        wait_loop:      ")
  HardwareHelp_L("                        in      a,(countlow)  ;get current counter value")
  HardwareHelp_L("                        sub     c             ;check time elapsed")
  HardwareHelp_L("                        cp      b             ;waittime already elapsed?")
  HardwareHelp_L("                        jr      c,wait_loop   ;if not , loop")
  HardwareHelp_L("                        ret")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Timer do sistema (Turbo-R) - System timer", "I/O-Port map")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("-------------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Chakkari Copy hardware description")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("-------------------------------------------------------------------------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This document describes the functionality of the Chakkari Copy cartridge hardware,")
  HardwareHelp_L("created by reverse engineering the 8KB ROM.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("+--------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("|          0000-3FFF: 16 KB RAM in COPY mode |")
  HardwareHelp_L("| Page 0:  available in COPY mode only       |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|          can be toggled to read-only       |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+--------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("|          4000-5FFF: 8KB ROM code           |")
  HardwareHelp_L("| Page 1:  +                                 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|          6000-67FF: 2KB internal RAM       |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+--------------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The shown 16KB RAM in page 0 are only available in COPY mode, but can be toggled to read-only.")
  HardwareHelp_L("The location of the RAM in RAM mode is probably page 2 (but could not be confirmed, so it's just a guess).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The memory at 6800-7FFF is unused by the cartridge.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Internal handling: during cartridge initialisation Chakkari Copy copies the contents of BIOS")
  HardwareHelp_L("page 0 into it's own RAM and patches some entries to capture the input of PAUSE and COPY buttons of the")
  HardwareHelp_L("cartridge. Afterwards the patched memory is set to read-only.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PAUSE and COPY itself are processed by the routines in the ROM. In case the page 0 is (for any reason)")
  HardwareHelp_L("switched to the original MSX-BIOS  the cartridge buttons are no longer working. In this case")
  HardwareHelp_L("CALL SCHANGE switches the BIOS to the patched version again.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In case a program switches screen modes in a way that prevents Chakkara Copy from detecting")
  HardwareHelp_L("the correct parameters Chakkara Copy can be started by keeping the COPY button pressed. In this")
  HardwareHelp_L("case the screen-mode autodetect will be skipped and the parameters can be entered manually.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This appears to be an undocumented feature, confirmed by typos and a very rudimentary screen handling.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In case the printer is not ready or not available the printing can be aborted by pressing Ctrl+Stop.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The Chakkari Copy cartridge has one register, which controls it's complete behavior.")
  HardwareHelp_L("This register is available at MSX port &H7F.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("READ only:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-----------------------------------------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("|  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  | PSE | CPY |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CPY: 0 = Copy button pressed, 1 = Copy button released")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PSE: 0 = Pause button pressed, 1 = Pause button released")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("WRITE only:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("|  1  |  1  |  1  |  1  | RAM | PSE | CPY |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CPY: 0 = Copy LED on, 1 = Copy LED off")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PSE: 0 = Pause LED on, 1 = Pause LED off")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("RAM: 0 = Page 0 in RAM mode, 1 = Page 0 in ROM mode (applies to COPY mode only)")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Chakkari Copy - MSX-BIOS: Chakkari Copy", "I/O-Port map")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("A8 | I/O | PPI port A: slotselect-register (2 bits per page/slot)Page 3  Page 2  Page 1  Page 0PSLOT#  PSLOT#  PSLOT#  PSLOT#")
  HardwareHelp_L("A9 | I | PPI port B: keyboard column input")
  HardwareHelp_L("AA | I/O | PPI port C: keyclick, CapsLED, tape, keyboardBits 0-3 = Keyboard row select")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 4 = CasMotor (0=on, 1=off)Bit 5 = CasOutBit 6 = Caps LED (0=on, 1=off)")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 7 = KeyClick")
  HardwareHelp_L("AB | O | PPI mode port:can be used to directly set or reset any bit of port C, when bit 7 is 0.")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 0 = set or reset bitBits 1-3 = bit number (0-7)Bits 4-6: not used")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 7: must be 0 tp perform the single bit set/reset functionis used to set the operating mode of the PPI, when bit 7 is 1.")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 0 = C Dir; direction of lower four bits only of port C: 0=Output (MSX), 1=Input")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 1 = B Dir; direction of Port B: 0=Output, 1=Input (MSX)")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 2 = B&C Mode; operating mode of Port B and the lower bits only of Port C: 0=Normal mode (MSX), 1=Strobed mode")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 3 = C Dir; direction of the upper four bits only of Port C: 0=Output (MSX), 1=Input")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 4 = A Dir; direction of Port A: 0=Output (MSX), 1=Input")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bits 5-6 = A&C Mode; operating mode of Port A and the upper four bits only of Port C: 00=Normal mode (MSX), 01=Strobed mode, 01=Bidirectional mode")
  HardwareHelp_L("      Bit 7: must be 1 in order to alter the PPI mode")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("PPI (8255) - PPI (Programmable Peripheral Interface)", "I/O-Port map")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("The S1990 chip is used to switch between different CPU's and CPU-modes (available since Turbo-R).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("&HE4: | Register write (write only)")
  HardwareHelp_L("&HE5: | Register contents (read/write)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The S1990 seems to:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("increment the register-number automatically, after a value was written")
  HardwareHelp_L("keep register-number unchanged, after a value was read")
  HardwareHelp_L("have 16 registers available for access.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Registers:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("5 | &H00=right&H40=left (front-switch active)")
  HardwareHelp_L("     Note: the front-switch is usually set to boot the firmware instead of MSX-Basic")
  HardwareHelp_L("6 | Bit 5: Processor more (0=R800, 1=Z80)")
  HardwareHelp_L("     Bit 6: ROM mode (0=ROM, 1=DRAM)")
  HardwareHelp_L("      &H00=R800 DRAM")
  HardwareHelp_L("      &H40=R800 ROM")
  HardwareHelp_L("      &H60=Z80")
  HardwareHelp_L("13 | ? (3 perhaps a primary slot # ?)")
  HardwareHelp_L("14 | Contains last but one byte written to memory")
  HardwareHelp_L("15 | Contains last byte written to memory")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Note: MSX engines other than S1990 behaves different compared to S1990 (for Turbo-R, of course). Engines other than S1990 never waits until the next VDP command arrives. S1990 waits.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("It is rumoured the turboR R800 mode's VDP slowdown can be disabled through one of the S1990's registers. ")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("S1990", "I/O-Port map")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("Here we explain mostly about how to access Kanji-ROM")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Cautions upon use of Kanji driver")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("MSX-BIOS: Kanji ROM", "Kanji ROM's")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("2. 4. 1 Input/output of Kanji ROM")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If you want to access kanji ROM on MSX, directly access D8H to DBH of I/O.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To read a kanji font data, you should use 12-bit data called kanji codes. These codes are different with JIS codes and kuten codes, so you should find out the kanji code of the one you want to display.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("#You can convert a kanji into JIS code by executing CALL JIS in kanji mode.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CALL JIS (A$,kanjistring$)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("then A$ will be a string of 4-digit hexadecimal. Kanjistring$ can be any long, but what you get is always a JIS of the first character.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("How to Calculate a Kanji Code")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Deduct 32 from the upper byte of a kanji's JIS code and call the value as ku. Deduct 32 from the lower byte of a kanji's JIS code and call the value as ten. (these are so-called kuten codes).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In case of 1st class kanjis, if ku is 15 or less, a kanji code is ku*96+ten. If ku is 16 or greater, a kanji code is ku*96+ten-512.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In case of 2nd class kanjis, a kanji code is ((ku-48)*96+ten).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Then, access I/O port using the kanji code you calculated. This differs depending on 1st and 2nd class.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To Read a 1st Class Kanji")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Output lower 6 bit of a kanji code to D8H of I/O port, and upper 6 bit to D9H. And read the value 32 times uninterrupted from D9H to get the font for the kanji.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To Read a 2nd Class Kanji")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Output lower 6 bit of a kanji code to DAH of I/O port, and upper 6 bit to DBH. And read the value 32 times uninterrupted from DBH to get the font for the kanji.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Font data are arranged this way. First, 8 bytes for upper-left, then 8 bytes for upper-right. Then 8 bytes for lower-left, and 8 bytes for lower-right.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Order of Data")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1st to 8th    A")
  HardwareHelp_L("9th to 16th   B")
  HardwareHelp_L("17th to 24th  C")
  HardwareHelp_L("25th to 32th  D")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Font Dots")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1                     9")
  HardwareHelp_L("to    A        B     to")
  HardwareHelp_L("8                    16")
  HardwareHelp_L("17                   25")
  HardwareHelp_L("to    C        D     to")
  HardwareHelp_L("24                   32")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Usually, direct input/output to I/O port is forbidden. But there is no other way in case of kanji ROM access. BASIC command PUT KANJI and BIOS called KNJPRT, which were added for MSX2 and later only supports 1st Class kanjis (MSX2+ and later supports 2nd class too) and also useless when you want to directly tamper with kanji fonts, say, when you want to compress 16*16 dot fonts into 12*16 dots, or when you want to directly print out the kanji font as bit images. Also, they do not support screen 2 or 4. And, there is no BIOS to access kanji ROM available in MSX1. Therefore, we access I/O port directly in case of kanji ROM access.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Beside, as many of you might know, if you access blank part of kanji ROM, you do not get a blank space but a font of kanij you didn't want to at all. To get a 2-byte space, make sure to use that of JIS code 2121H. About hankaku letters (narrow katakanas, alphabets and puctuations), use only the left part (A and C in the diagram up) of fonts between JIS code 2021H to 207EH, and 2921H to 295FH. These hankaku fonts are not supported by kanji drivers and PUT KANJI command.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2. 4. 2 Existence of Kanji ROM")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If you try kanji input/output without kanji ROM, bean curds (16*16 dots white square) appear on the screen and you will feel really sorry. Therefore, calibrating the existence of kanji ROM becomes necessary.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To Check Whether 1st Class Kanji ROM Exist")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("First 8 bytes of the font with JIS code 2140H (ku-1, ten-32) of the kanji ROM is always in order of 00H, 40H, 20H, 10H, 08H, 04H, 02H, 01H. This means that, if you read the out font for this code, and if all first 8 bytes correspond to these, there must be 1st Class kanji ROM present.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To Check Whether 2nd Class Kanji ROM Exist")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Read out the font data of the one with JIS code 737EH (ku-83, ten-94), sum up the values of first 8 bytes and divide it by 256, and if mod is 149 (95H), there must be 2nd Class kanji ROM present.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When making a software, make sure it checks the existence of kanji ROM, and to make it available on machines without kanji ROMs, prepare 1-byte messages. A software which uses tons of rare kanjis on 2nd-Class equipped machines and display simple alternative kanjis on machines without is possible, but I have never seen such.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2. 4. 3 SHIFT JIS Code")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In MSX, kanji files use SHIFT JIS code (Microsoft kanji code). This is because since in MS-DOS kanji files are standardized by Microsoft kanji code, so on MSX which use same format, same codes are used.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("In shift JIS code, there are no kanji start and end codes, and you can use both hankaku and 2 byte kanjis together.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Here, we present an algorithm to convert from JIS code into SHIFT JIS.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Assume the upper byte of a JIS code as JIH, and lower byte as JIL. And assume upper byte of SHIFT JIS code as SJH, and lower byte as SJL.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To Convert from JIS Code into SHIFT JIS Code")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1. If JIH is 5EH or below,  INT((JIH-1)/2+71H) is SJH. Otherwise, INT((JIH-1)/2+B1H) is SJH..")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2. If JIH is even number, (JIL+7EH) is SJL. Otherwise, temporary suppose (JIL+1FH) as SJL. If this value is 7FH or more, add 1 to it. If it is smaller than 7FH, leave it as is.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To convert from SHIFT JIS into JIS")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("1. If SJH is smaller than A0H, subtract 71H from SJH and name it as H. If it is more than A0H, subtract B1H from SJH and name it as H instead. Whichever the case, substitute H*2+1 into H again.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("2. If SJL is greater than 7FH, then SJL-1 is L. Otherwise, SJL itself is. Then, if L is smaller than 9EH, L-1FH becomes L. If L is 9EH or more, L-7DH becomes L, and H+1 becomes H.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("	Now value of H is upper byte of JIS code, and L is the lower byte of it.")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Acessando a ROM Kanji - About how to access Kanji ROM", "Kanji ROM's")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("Chapter 8 Cautions upon Use of Kanji Driver")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Refer to " + Chr(34) + "MSX2+ Powerful Application" + Chr(34) + " from ASCII Corp. to see how to use kanji driver. Here we explain information not noted in the document.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("When kanji driver is active, screen is put into SCREEN 5 or 7 status. This means, when kanji driver is active, even if you execute something like SCREEN 0, the change will be limited to number of characters per line; screen remains in graphic mode. If you execute, say, SCREEN 5 in this state, you will enter what is called kanji graphic mode, where you can use LINE or PAINT statements normally, while at the same time you can display kanjis with PRINT statements. However, you cannot input kanjis in this mode. (your can do so by coding your own handmade machine code program, though).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Other things you need to pay attention include, that once a kanji driver is booted, free area decreases by 4 kilobytes . (type of MSX-JE you booted might result in decrease of less than 4 kilobytes. But make sure to reserve 4 kilobytes for kanji driver when you program, for safety purpose.) This means, if a software is using memory to limit, it might malfunction once it boots kanji driver. And one more; all contents of program stacks will be cleared on the first occasion you boot kanji driver using CALL KANJI statement. This means, information like the original line number you were before jumping out with GOSUB statement, and statuses and variable contents of FOR-to-NEXT loops, will be altogether deleted. It is recommended to execute CALL KANJI on first lines in programs which use kanji driver.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("And, you will find yourself that you can use kanjis for file names when kanji driver is active. But if you do so, then files with their names which you can never call in non-kanji modes happen to be created; so avoid using kanjis for file names. For instance, you will get a " + Chr(34) + "Bad file name" + Chr(34) + " error if you try to direct a file beginning with a kanji " + Chr(34) + "yo" + Chr(34) + " (4024H in JIS kanji code) in non-kanji mode. (but DOS kernel of Ver. 2.31 or later does not regard this as an error)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("We introduce you hooks which a kanji driver overwrites, for your notice. Be careful when your program uses following hooks.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("FDA4H(H.CHPU),FDA9H(H.DSPC)")
  HardwareHelp_L("FDAEH(H.ERAC),FDB3H(H.DSPF)")
  HardwareHelp_L("FDB8H(H.ERAF),FDBDH(H.TOTE)")
  HardwareHelp_L("FDC2H(H.CHGE),FDDBH(H.PINL)")
  HardwareHelp_L("FDE5H(H.INLI),FFB6H(H.LPTO)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If one of these hooks is called, then contents of " + Chr(34) + "hidden" + Chr(34) + " registers and IX and IY registers are destroyed. Therefore, a software which expects contents of registers stay as they are, does not run properly if you boot kanji driver.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Note, in addition to above, there are more hooks which a kanji driver overwrites, as noted below. I am not sure whether hidden registers and index registers stay preserved or not when these hooks are called. Probably they will be preserved. But I cannot answer, since I'm not an ASCII person ;)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("FD9FH(H.TIMI),FF84H(H,WIDT)")
  HardwareHelp_L("FFC0H(H.SCRE),FFCAH(FCALL)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("--------------Out-of-space article--------------")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("About locations of pattern name tables when kanji driver is in use; they are placed on following VRAM addresses.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN 2    1E20H+40*Y+X")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("(if you equip MSX1 machine with kanji driver, then screen 2 or 3 is chosen)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN 3    0B00H+16*Y+X")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN 4    1B00H+40*Y+X    (0<=Y<=2)")
  HardwareHelp_L("    1B08H+40*Y+X    (3<=Y<=11)")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN 5    (6A00H+40*Y+X+RG23SA*128) AND 7FFFH")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN 6    (6A00H+80*Y+X+RG23SA*128) AND 7FFFH")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN 7    D400H+80*Y+X+RG23SA*256 AND FFFFH")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCREEN 8    D400H+40*Y+X+RG23SA*256 AND FFFFH")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Normally, one of SCREEN 5 to 7 is used. (refer to kanji BASIC manual) Since kanji driver utilizes hardware scrolls, so pattern name tables slither like slugs;) for each time a scroll occur.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Screen 2 or 3 is used when you equip MSX1 machine with a kanji driver.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Takamichi's note: what is " + Chr(34) + "pattern name table? ^^;")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("About CALL KANJI statement and screen modes which a MSX enters into then.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("About size of one kanji character;")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CALL KANJI016 x 16 pixels (default)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CALL KANJI1Standard MSX kanji ROM is used. Characters are compressed to 12 x 16 pixels.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CALL KANJI2Same as in CALL KANJI 0. But vertical compression occurs due to interlace mode, so lines you can display increase.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CALL KANJI3Same as in CALL KANJI 1. But vertical compression occurs due to interlace mode, so lines you can display increase.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("About screen modes;")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CALL KANJI0 or CALL KANJI2 ... SCREEN 5 if WIDTH statement states 20 to 32 characters per line, SCREEN 7 if it states 33 to 64 characters per line.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CALL KANJI1 or CALL KANJI3 ... SCREEN 5 if WIDTH statement states 20 to 40 characters per line, SCREEN 7 if it states 41 to 80 characters per line.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("(I don't know when screen 8 is chosen. Maybe it's possible only by executing SCREEN 8 statement after entering kanji mode using CALL KANJI)")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Usando o driver Kanji - Cautions upon use of Kanji driver", "Kanji ROM's")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("6.3.6 PCM Sound")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Users are permitted to access turboR PCM using I/O ports.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If you control PCM by yourself, you can change sampling rates to any frequency you desire, or replay the recorded sound data through different frequency to achieve special sound effect. Here we would like to describe how to control I/O ports directly to handle PCMs. See later for BIOSes.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I/O Ports Specifications;")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("I/O Port Nos. bit7 bit6 bit5 bit4 bit3 bit2 bit1 bit0")
  HardwareHelp_L("A4H (read)    0    0    0    0    0    0    CT1  CT0")
  HardwareHelp_L("A4H (write)   DA7  DA6  DA5  DA4  DA3  DA2  DA1  DA0")
  HardwareHelp_L("A5H (read)    COMP 0    0    SMPL SEL  FILT MUTE ADDA")
  HardwareHelp_L("A5H (write)   0    0    0    SMPL SEL  FILT MUTE ADDA")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Summary;")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CT1, CT0 (counter data)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("These counters counts every 63.5 microseconds. PCM BIOSes reads/writes sync to these counters. Once you write a value into A4H of I/O, these counters will be cleared.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DA'7 to DA0 (D/A output data)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PCM data will be played when you write a 8-bit data here. Format of the data is absolute binary; 127 is equal to 0 level (see my description about BASIC statements for what the " + Chr(34) + "level" + Chr(34) + " is). In times of recording, MSX outputs a value here and checks recorded data by learning whether the value is greater or lesser than the coming data.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("COMP (comparator output signal)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("MSX compares sample hold output signal and D/A converter output signal. If D/A output is smaller than sample/hold output, this COMP bit stands up. If D/A output is equal or greater than sample/hold output, COMP bit does not stand up. Use this at times of recording.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SMPL (sample/hold signal)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Use this to select whether MSX will sample the input signal, or hold the value. If the input value changes while very time you are checking the value itself;), there will be a big trouble, so you should keep this SMPL bit at 1 when you are in middle of checking input value.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SEL (selection of filter input signal)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Use this bit to decide whether D/A converter output or an output signal from microphone amplifier is used as input signal to low path filter. If SEL bit is 0, then D/A converter output signal is used; if 1, then microphone amplifier output signal is used.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("FILT (selection of sample/hold circuit input signal)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Use this bit to decide whether filter output signal or standard signal is used as input signal to sample hold circuit at times of A/D. If FILT bit is 0, then standard signal (default after resetting an MSX); if 1, filter output signal.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("MUTE (muting control)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This signal is responsible for turning on/off sound output over whole system. If 0, then sound output is off (default after resetting an MSX), while 1 means sound output is on.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("ADDA (buffer mode)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This bits specifies D/A converter output mode. Specify 0 (double buffer; a default after resetting an MSX) to do D/A, while you should specify 1 (single buffer) to do A/D.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To play a PCM sound, output 03H to address A5H, and continuously output the data from memory to address A4H. Use system timer wisely to sync well during such output. By using counters at A4H, you can use 4 types of frequencies supported by BIOS to play. It will be even better if you do not use these counters but delicately changes frequencies relying on system timers, and adjust the tone pitch while playing.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("To record a PCM sound, firstly, output 06H to address A5H (a startup, to turn FILT and MUTE to 1), then")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("<< output 16H to A5H (SMPL is 1), 80H to A4H, and immediately read a value of A5H. If bit 7 of the value is standing (=if COMP bit is standing), it means signal in question is 80H or more, so add 40H to output value 80H, and output this value to A4H. If bit7 is not standing, it means signal in question is less than 80H, so deduct 40H from the output value 80H, and output the value back again to A4H. Next, read value of A5H again, and check if bit7 is standing or not, and add/subtract 20H to the output value, and output the result value back to A4H, then read A5H, check bit7, add/subtract 10H from ... (snip) ... repeat such procedures 8 times, that's what you are required to perform 1 byte of data. Save this value into the memory, and output 03H to A5H >>")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("and adjust sync using system timer or something, repeat all the process enclosed by <<>>, read next 1 byte, then next byte (snip)... recording is done this way.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If you allocate sampling data into VRAM, and if you use different screen modes at times of recording and playing, you may suffer incorrect playing. This happens because MSX VRAM allotment varies upon screen modes. While I recommend you to see other sources for details, I simply explain, that 0001H address in SCREEN5 and 0001H address in SCREEN7 are different locations.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Routines to deal with PCM routines:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PCMPLY (0186H/MainROM) - play PCM sound")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("PCMREC (0189H/MainROM) - record PCM sound")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Refer to: MainROM")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("MSX-BIOS: PCM", "PCM")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("The MSX MIDI I/O ports (&HE8 and &HE9) are available since MSX Turbo-R:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("E8 (read)  |RXD7|RXD6|RXD5|RXD4|RXD3|RXD2|RXD1|RXD0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("E8 (write) |TXD7|TXD6|TXD5|TXD4|TXD3|TXD2|TXD1|TXD0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("RXD7-RXD0  =  8251 receive data")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("TXD7-TXD0  =  8251 send data")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("E9 (read)  |DSR |BRK | FE | OE | PE |EMPT|RDDY|RTDY|")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The first data that is written after a reset into the command register")
  HardwareHelp_L("is the mode instruction. The mode instruction looks as following:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("           | S2 | S1 | EP |PEN | L2 | L1 | B2 | B1 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("B2, B1 | bit 1,0 | 00 = synchrone mode")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 01 = baudrate factor is 1")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 10 = baudrate factor is 16")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 11 = baudrate factor is 64")
  HardwareHelp_L("L2, L1 | bit 3,2 | 00 = 5 data bits")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 01 = 6 data bits")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 10 = 7 data bits")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 11 = 8 data bits")
  HardwareHelp_L("PEN | bit 4 | 0 = no parity")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = parity present")
  HardwareHelp_L("EP | bit 5 | 0 = no even parity")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = even parity")
  HardwareHelp_L("(not necessary if PEN = 0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("S2, S1 | bit 7,6 | 00 = no stop")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 01 = 1 stop bit")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 10 = 1.5 stop bit")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 11 = 2 stop bits")
  HardwareHelp_L(" (valid for the sender. The receiver always has 1 stop bit)")
  HardwareHelp_L("Indien | bit 1,0 | 00 = synchrone mode, in this case bits 6 and 7 have the following meaning:")
  HardwareHelp_L(" ")
  HardwareHelp_L("  ")
  HardwareHelp_L("   bit 6 | ESD | 0 = internal synchronisation")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1 = external synchronisation")
  HardwareHelp_L("bit 7 | SCS | 0 = sync. character")
  HardwareHelp_L("   1 = synch. characters")
  HardwareHelp_L("   (the sync characters have to be written immediately after the mode instruction")
  HardwareHelp_L("   into the command register)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" ")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("After the mode instruction the command instruction can be written")
  HardwareHelp_L("at any desired time.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The mode instruction looks as following:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("           | EH | IR |RIE | ER |SBRK| RE |TIE |TEN |")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("EH | 0 = no effect")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = hunt mode")
  HardwareHelp_L("IR | 0 = no effect")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = internal reset")
  HardwareHelp_L("RIE | RTS?")
  HardwareHelp_L("ER | 0 = no effect")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = reset error flags PE,OE,FE")
  HardwareHelp_L("SBRK | 0 = no effect")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = send break character")
  HardwareHelp_L("PE | 0 = disable recipent")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = enable recipient")
  HardwareHelp_L("TIE | CTS?")
  HardwareHelp_L("TEN | 0 = disable sender")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = enable sender")
  HardwareHelp_L("For initialisation of in- and output of the MIDI port the following")
  HardwareHelp_L("bytes have to be written to port &H9E: 00,00,00,40.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The program sees")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("8253 Out2 interrupt (&HEA, &HEB):")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("EA (write) | -  | -  | -  | -  | -  | -  | -  | -  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Interrupt, reset")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("8253 counters (&HEC-&HEF):")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("EF (read)  |SC1 |SC0 |RW1 |RW0 | M2 | M2 | M0 |BCD |")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("SC1,SC0 | 00 = select counter 0")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 01 = select counter 1")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 10 = select counter 2")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 11 = not permitted")
  HardwareHelp_L("RW1,RW0 | 00 = latch counter")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 01 = read/write MSB only")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 01 = read/write LSB only")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 11 = read/write LSB and MSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("M2,M1,M0 | 000 = mode 0 (interrupt on terminal count, time out of a modem)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 001 = mode 1 (programmable one shot)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" X10 = mode 2 (rate generator, real time clock)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" X11 = mode 3 (blockgolf generator, baudrate generator)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 100 = mode 4 (software triggered strobe)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 101 = mode 5 (hardware triffered strobe, pulse-width measuring)")
  HardwareHelp_L("BCD | 0 = binary counting")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 1 = BCD counting")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("EC (R/W)   |CT7 |CT6 |CT5 |CT4 |CT3 |CT2 |CT1 |CT0 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("ED (R/W)   |CT17|CT16|CT15|CT14|CT13|CT12|CT11|CT10|")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("ED (R/W)   |CT27|CT26|CT25|CT24|CT23|CT22|CT21|CT20|")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("CT7-CT0 | data counter 0")
  HardwareHelp_L("CT17-CT10 | data counter 1")
  HardwareHelp_L("CT27-CT20 | data counter 2")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("E2 (write) | EN | -  | -  | -  | -  | -  | -  | E8 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("EN | MIDI interface on/off (0=on, 1=off)")
  HardwareHelp_L("E8 | MIDI interface on E8/E9 or E0/E1 (0=E8/E9, 1=E0/E1)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("E0 (read)  |RXD7|RXD6|RXD5|RXD4|RXD3|RXD2|RXD1|RXD0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("E0 (write) |TXD7|TXD6|TXD5|TXD4|TXD3|TXD2|TXD1|TXD0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("RXD7-RXD0  =  8251 receive data")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("TXD7-TXD0  =  8251 send data")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("E1 (read)  |DSR |BRK | FE | OE | PE |EMPT|RDDY|RTDY|")
  HardwareHelp_L("           +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("DSR | 8253 data set ready")
  HardwareHelp_L("BRK | 8251 sync detection")
  HardwareHelp_L("FE | 8251 framing error")
  HardwareHelp_L("OE | 8251 overrun error")
  HardwareHelp_L("PE | 8251 parity error")
  HardwareHelp_L("EMPT | 8251 transmitter empty")
  HardwareHelp_L("RDDY | 8251 receiver ready")
  HardwareHelp_L("TRDY | 8251 transmitter ready")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Check MIDI:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Available since Turbo-R (verify byte on 002DH of the Main-ROM,")
  HardwareHelp_L("which must be have at least the value 3).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Internal MSX MIDI of U-Pack (no FM-Basic)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Address | internal | external")
  HardwareHelp_L("4018 | 41 (A) | ?? (?)")
  HardwareHelp_L("4019 | 50 (P) | ?? (?)")
  HardwareHelp_L("401A | 52 (R) | ?? (?)")
  HardwareHelp_L("401B | 4C (L) | ?? (?)")
  HardwareHelp_L("401C | 4F (O) | 4D (M)")
  HardwareHelp_L("401D | 50 (P) | 49 (I)")
  HardwareHelp_L("401E | 4C (L) | 44 (D)")
  HardwareHelp_L("401F | 4C (L) | 49 (I)")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("MSX-BIOS: MIDI", "MIDI")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("The WD33C93A is the SCSI-Controller chip used in many SCSI-Interfaces")
  HardwareHelp_L("for the MSX (e.g. HSH and NOVAXIS). This document describes only")
  HardwareHelp_L("features that are specific to the WD33C93A and does not contain")
  HardwareHelp_L("any (public) SCSI specification!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Register select / read auxilary status (&H34)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Writing to this port (#34) selects a register of the WD33C93A,")
  HardwareHelp_L("which can be written or read by accessing port #35.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Reading from this port (#34) returns the auxilary status of the WD33C93A.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Read/write register (&H35)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Writing into this port writes to the WD33C93A register,")
  HardwareHelp_L("selected in port (#34). Reading from this port returns")
  HardwareHelp_L("the contents of the corresponding register.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("After every access to this port the register-number")
  HardwareHelp_L("is automatically incremented by one, except for the following")
  HardwareHelp_L("locations: Auxilary Status Register ($1F), Data Register ($19) and the")
  HardwareHelp_L("Command Register ($18).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Reset (&H36)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Writing any(!) byte to port #36 resets the WD33C93A.")
  HardwareHelp_L("The BSY-flag in the status register is set until the reset is finished!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Undocumented(?) feature: reading from this port seems to return some sort of")
  HardwareHelp_L("status bits. This is essential for the functionality, but the association of")
  HardwareHelp_L("these bits is not known yet! (probably some output of the QueueTag?).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Unknown: are connected devices reset too?")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Note: after the reset is finished the WD33C93 contains the value $00 in its " + Chr(34) + "CDB 1" + Chr(34) + " register ($03).")
  HardwareHelp_L("The WD33C93A and WD33C93B are containing the value $01. The difference between the two")
  HardwareHelp_L("chips is that the WD33C93B provides the " + Chr(34) + "Queue tag" + Chr(34) + " register ($1A), where the WD33C93A doesnt.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Registers:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The WD33C93A has 32 registers with the following meaning:")
  HardwareHelp_L("Register | Contents")
  HardwareHelp_L("0 | 00 | OWN ID/CDB SIZE REGISTER")
  HardwareHelp_L("  ")
  HardwareHelp_L("  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("  |FS1|FS0| 0 |EHP|EAF|ID2|ID1|ID0|")
  HardwareHelp_L("  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 0-2: IDn SCSI ID Bits 0-2 set the SCSI bus ID number")
  HardwareHelp_L("that the WD33C93A will use during arbitration and selection.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 3: EAF ENABLE ADVANCED FEATURES, when set to one, causes the")
  HardwareHelp_L("WD33C93A to enable certain advanced features. When this bit is")
  HardwareHelp_L("zero those features are disabled.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 4: EHP ENABLE HOST PARITY, when set to one, enables odd parity")
  HardwareHelp_L("checking on the host bus; the PE bit in the Auxilary status register")
  HardwareHelp_L("will indicate parity errors detected on the host bus, and the HHP bit")
  HardwareHelp_L("in the Control register will be used. When this bit is zero, no checking")
  HardwareHelp_L("is performed on the host bus: the PE bit is not set when a parity error")
  HardwareHelp_L("is detected on the host bus, and the HHP bit must be set to zero.")
  HardwareHelp_L("NOTE: Parity is always generated on the host parity bit (DP), regardless")
  HardwareHelp_L("of the state of this bit.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 5: RAF, not used, will be zero.")
  HardwareHelp_L("=$00: FS 8")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 6: FS 12")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 7: FS 16")
  HardwareHelp_L("FSn FREQUENCY SELECT 0-1 select the divisor that is applied to the")
  HardwareHelp_L("input clock. The resulting clock is used for data transfer timing and for")
  HardwareHelp_L("SCSI bus arbitration timing. The table below shows input clock frequency")
  HardwareHelp_L("ranges and the corresponding divisors. The correct divisor for the input")
  HardwareHelp_L("clock must be used, or SCSI bus timing specifications may not met.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-------------+-----+-----+-----------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| INPUT CLOCK |     |     | RESULTING |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|  FREQUENCY  | FS1 | FS0 |  DIVISOR  |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|    (MHZ)    |     |     |           |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-------------+-----+-----+-----------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("|    8 - 10   |  0  |  0  |     2     |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|   12 - 15   |  0  |  1  |     3     |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|      16     |  1  |  0  |     4     |")
  HardwareHelp_L("|      xx     |  1  |  1  | undefined |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-------------+-----+-----+-----------+")
  HardwareHelp_L("Note that an 11 HMZ clock rate should not be used, as the resulting SCSI bus")
  HardwareHelp_L("cleay delay may violate SCSI specifications. The formula for computing the")
  HardwareHelp_L("maximum SCSI data transfer rate is:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("                             INPUT CLOCK FREQUENCY")
  HardwareHelp_L("MAXIMUM SCSI TRANSFER RATE = --------------------- [MByte/sec]")
  HardwareHelp_L("                                 CLOCK DIVISOR")
  HardwareHelp_L("1 | 01 | CONTROL REGISTER")
  HardwareHelp_L("  ")
  HardwareHelp_L("  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("  |DM2|DM1|DM0|HHP|EDI|IDI|HA |HSP|")
  HardwareHelp_L("  +---+---+---+---+---+---+---+---+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 0: HSP The HALT on SCSI PARITY ERROR bit enables the WD33C93A to terminate a Receive")
  HardwareHelp_L("or Transfer command if a parity error is detected on an incoming SCSI data byte. In the")
  HardwareHelp_L("Initiator role, termination due to a SSI parity error causes the ACK- pin to be the left")
  HardwareHelp_L("in the active state in order to inhibit any additional data transfers (REQs) by the Target;")
  HardwareHelp_L("this facilitates error handling with Target. Synchronous data transfers check parity every")
  HardwareHelp_L("4096 bytes, or at the end when the remaining transfer count, whichever is less.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Asynchronous transfers check parity on every byte.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 1: HA The HALT on ATTENTION bit (in Target mode only) enables the WD33C93A to terminate")
  HardwareHelp_L("a Send or Receive command if the ATN- input is asserted. This normally indicates that the")
  HardwareHelp_L("Initiator detected a parity error while receiving data from the WD33C93A. The ATN- input is")
  HardwareHelp_L("tested before the start of the data-transfer, every 4096 bytes if the transfer count is")
  HardwareHelp_L("greater than 4096, and after the end of the transfer. These rules apply to both synchronous")
  HardwareHelp_L("and asynchronous transfers.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 2: IDI")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 3: EDI")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 4: HHP")
  HardwareHelp_L("=$00: POLLED")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 5: BURST")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 6: BUS")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 7: DMA")
  HardwareHelp_L("2 | 02 | Timeout period")
  HardwareHelp_L("Timeout is value*10 ms (e.g. 125*10=1250ms)")
  HardwareHelp_L("3 | 03 | CDB 1")
  HardwareHelp_L("4 | 04 | CDB 2")
  HardwareHelp_L("5 | 05 | CDB 3")
  HardwareHelp_L("6 | 06 | CDB 4")
  HardwareHelp_L("7 | 07 | CDB 5")
  HardwareHelp_L("8 | 08 | CDB 6")
  HardwareHelp_L("9 | 09 | CDB 7")
  HardwareHelp_L("10 | 0A | CDB 8")
  HardwareHelp_L("11 | 0B | CDB 9")
  HardwareHelp_L("12 | 0C | CDB 10")
  HardwareHelp_L("13 | 0D | CDB 11")
  HardwareHelp_L("14 | 0E | CDB 12")
  HardwareHelp_L("15 | 0F | Target LUN")
  HardwareHelp_L("16 | 10 | Command phase")
  HardwareHelp_L("17 | 11 | Synchronous transfer")
  HardwareHelp_L("18 | 12 | Transfer count (MSB)")
  HardwareHelp_L("19 | 13 | Transfer count")
  HardwareHelp_L("20 | 14 | Transfer count (LSB)")
  HardwareHelp_L("21 | 15 | Destination id")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 6: DPD")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 7: SCC")
  HardwareHelp_L("22 | 16 | Source id")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 0-2: id")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 3: SIV")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 5: DSP")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 6: ES")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 7: ER")
  HardwareHelp_L("23 | 17 | SCSI status")
  HardwareHelp_L("$00 - Good Status.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$02 - Check Condition.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$04 - Condition Met/Good.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$08 - Busy.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$10 - Intermediate/Good.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$14 - Intermediate/Condition Met/Good.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$18 - Reservation Conflict.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$22 - Command Terminated.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$28 - Queue Full.")
  HardwareHelp_L("$xx - Other Codes Are Reserved")
  HardwareHelp_L("24 | 18 | Command")
  HardwareHelp_L("$00 Reset")
  HardwareHelp_L("$01 Abort")
  HardwareHelp_L("$02 Assert ATN")
  HardwareHelp_L("$03 Negate ACK")
  HardwareHelp_L("$04 Disconnect")
  HardwareHelp_L("$05 Reselect")
  HardwareHelp_L("$06 Select-With-ATN")
  HardwareHelp_L("$07 Select-Without-ATN")
  HardwareHelp_L("$08 Select-W/ATN-And-Transfer")
  HardwareHelp_L("$09 Select-w/o ATN-And-Transfer")
  HardwareHelp_L("$0A Reselect-And-Receive-Data")
  HardwareHelp_L("$0B Reselect-And-Send-Data")
  HardwareHelp_L("$0C Wait-For-Select-And-Receive")
  HardwareHelp_L("$0D Send-Status-And-Command-Complete")
  HardwareHelp_L("$0E Send-Disconnect-Message")
  HardwareHelp_L("$0F Set IDI")
  HardwareHelp_L("$10 Receive Command")
  HardwareHelp_L("$11 Receive Data")
  HardwareHelp_L("$12 Receive Message Out")
  HardwareHelp_L("$13 Receive Unspecified Info Out")
  HardwareHelp_L("$14 Send Status")
  HardwareHelp_L("$15 Send Data")
  HardwareHelp_L("$16 Send Message In")
  HardwareHelp_L("$17 Send Unspecified Info In")
  HardwareHelp_L("$18 Translate Address")
  HardwareHelp_L("$20 Transfer Info")
  HardwareHelp_L("25 | 19 | Data")
  HardwareHelp_L("26 | 1A | Queue tag (not available in WD33C93A?)")
  HardwareHelp_L("27 | 1B")
  HardwareHelp_L("28 | 1C")
  HardwareHelp_L("29 | 1D")
  HardwareHelp_L("30 | 1E")
  HardwareHelp_L("31 | 1F | Auxiliary status")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Auxilary Status:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The Auxilary STATUS-register of the WD33C93A is read-only can be accessed by reading port #34.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Please do not confuse with the SCSI-Status, which can be retrieved from")
  HardwareHelp_L("the WD33C93A register #17.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Important: reading this status-register returns the current state and resets")
  HardwareHelp_L("the interrupt (INT) flag, therefore reading the status-register twice may")
  HardwareHelp_L("return different results (at least the INT-flag is zero in the 2nd query).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("| INT | LCI | BSY | CIP |  0  |  0  | PE  | DBR |")
  HardwareHelp_L("+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit | Mask | Meaning")
  HardwareHelp_L("0 | $01 | DBR | 1=data buffer ready (read from register #19)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  Note: if a command ends with no more data (0 bytes result or DBR switching from 1 to 0)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  an interrupt is generated (INT bit set) and the resultcode can be retrieved from")
  HardwareHelp_L("  the SCSI status (register #17).")
  HardwareHelp_L("  Data Buffer Ready is used during programmed I/O to indicate to the processor")
  HardwareHelp_L("  whether or not the Dta register is available for reading or writing. During")
  HardwareHelp_L("  Send or Transfer commands which transmit data oder the SCSI bus, the DBR bit")
  HardwareHelp_L("  is set when the WD33C93A is ready to take a byte from the host; the bit is")
  HardwareHelp_L("  reset when the processor writes the byte to the data-register. During Receive")
  HardwareHelp_L("  or Transfer commands which receive data over the SCSI bus the DBR is set when")
  HardwareHelp_L("  a byte is received; it is reset when the processor reads the byte from the")
  HardwareHelp_L("  DATA register.")
  HardwareHelp_L("1 | $02 | PE | Parity Error status indicates that even parity was detected on a data")
  HardwareHelp_L("  byte received during an information transfer. Parity is checked on data received")
  HardwareHelp_L("  from the host bus during transfers out to the SCSI bus, and is checked on data")
  HardwareHelp_L("  received from the SCSI bus during transfers out to the host bus. Detection of")
  HardwareHelp_L("  parity error will set the PE status bit regardless of the state of the HHP or HSP")
  HardwareHelp_L("  bits in the Control register. The PE bit is cleared when a new command is issued.")
  HardwareHelp_L("2 | $04 | Not used, will be zero.")
  HardwareHelp_L("3 | $08 | Not used, will be zero.")
  HardwareHelp_L("4 | $10 | CIP | COMMAND IN PROGRESS, when set, indicates that the WD33C93A is interpreting the")
  HardwareHelp_L("  last command entered into the Command register and therefore this register is unavailable.")
  HardwareHelp_L("  When this bit is reset, a command may be written into the Command register.")
  HardwareHelp_L("5 | $20 | BSY | 1=busy, 0=ready")
  HardwareHelp_L("  BUSY indicates that a Level II command is currently executing and therefore only the")
  HardwareHelp_L("  Command register(when CIP=0), the DATA register, and the Auxilary Status register are")
  HardwareHelp_L("  accessible by the host. A Level II command may not be written to the Command register")
  HardwareHelp_L("  when this bit is one.")
  HardwareHelp_L("6 | $40 | LCI | LAST COMMAND IGNORED indicates that a command was issued by the host just prior to")
  HardwareHelp_L("  or concurrent with a pending interrupt, and therefore the command will be ignored and")
  HardwareHelp_L("  this status set.")
  HardwareHelp_L("7 | $80 | INT | 1=interrupt, 0=no interrupt")
  HardwareHelp_L("  Note: if the interrupt is set the SCSI status (register #17) must be read!")
  HardwareHelp_L("  INTERRUPT PENDING indicates that the Intrq pin is asserted. The host should read the SCSI")
  HardwareHelp_L("  Status register to clear the Intrq prior to issuing any commands.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Lex Lechz: Unfortunately Western Digital does no longer sell the WD33C93A, nor")
  HardwareHelp_L("have copies of the original documentation. Therefore I want to thank Hans Otten for scanning and publishing a copy of")
  HardwareHelp_L("the manual. I also want to thank Manuel Bilderbeek for his effort to find a copy of this")
  HardwareHelp_L("manual.")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("SCSI: WD33C93A", "SCSI Controller (WD33C93A)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("The photoresistor attached to tip of the lightpen catches the scanline of the TV display,")
  HardwareHelp_L("and finds which part of the screen is pointed by the pen.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This documentation has been created by reverse-engineering the MSX2/2+ BIOS")
  HardwareHelp_L("and the firmware of the Sanyo Lightpen cartridge. Therefore not all information may")
  HardwareHelp_L("be 100% correct.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The MSX MIDI I/O ports (&HB8 to &HBB) are assigned to the Sanyo Lightpen interface,")
  HardwareHelp_L("which is available for MSX1 and standardized for MSX 2/2+:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Registers")
  HardwareHelp_L("Data registers")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("                 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("B8 (read/write)  | Y7 | Y6 | Y5 | Y4 | Y3 | Y2 | Y1 | Y0 |   DATA0")
  HardwareHelp_L("                 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("B9 (read/write)  | X6 | X5 | X4 | X3 | X2 | X1 | X0 | Y8 |   DATA1")
  HardwareHelp_L("                 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("BA (read/write)  | ?? | ?? |Vrfy|Posn|Butn| X9 | X8 | X7 |   DATA2")
  HardwareHelp_L("                 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Command/Mode register")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("                 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("BB (read/write)  | M7 | M6 | M5 | M4 | M3 | M2 | M1 | M0 |   CMND")
  HardwareHelp_L("                 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Y0-Y8 | Y-coordinate (0-511)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" Not calibrated, top-most pixel has usually position 38 ($26)")
  HardwareHelp_L("X0-X9 | X-coordinate (0-1023)")
  HardwareHelp_L(" Not calibrated, left-most pixel has usually position 44 ($2C)")
  HardwareHelp_L("Butn | 0 if button pressed")
  HardwareHelp_L("Posn | 0 if X/Y coordinates are known (lightpen position valid)")
  HardwareHelp_L("Vrfy | 0 if position verification was successful.")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  If 1 X/Y coordinates are invalid and have to be ignored")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  (same as if Posn=1).")
  HardwareHelp_L("M0-M7")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Interrupts")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The Lightpen hardware appears to be able to generate interrupts.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Where the MSX-BIOS does not use them the Sanyo Lightpen Interface")
  HardwareHelp_L("relies on these interrupts.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The interrupt handling is not documented yet!")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Operation")
  HardwareHelp_L("Get button-state/position")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Read DATA2-register.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("If Butn=0 the lightpen-button is pressed.")
  HardwareHelp_L(" ")
  HardwareHelp_L("If Posn=0 the lightpen-position is known and DATA0 and DATA1 can be read to get the coordinates.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Registers should be read in this order. Under some circumstances the coordinates may not be valid, therefore")
  HardwareHelp_L("a validation might be required (not always, but when?).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Retrieved coordinates are non-calibrated, therefore the position of the left-most and top-most pixel is most likely not zero.")
  HardwareHelp_L("The BIOS-variables XSAVE and YSAVE (valid since MSX2) are keeping the (calibrated) position of the left/top pixel.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Verify position")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This behavior is implemented in the Sanyo Lightpen Interface, but not in the MSX-BIOS:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Write retrieved (non-calibrated) coordinate-values X-18 and Y-3")
  HardwareHelp_L("into DATA0, DATA1, DATA2 (in this order). Bits that do not belong to coordinates should be")
  HardwareHelp_L("(need to be?) 0. Maybe a short cycle of some ms is necessary until the command is processed.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Then read DATA2 and check the contents of the Vrfy bit, which should be 0.")
  HardwareHelp_L("If 1 the coordinates are invalid and have to be ignored.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("MSX BIOS")
  HardwareHelp_L("Lightpen support is available on MSX2 and MSX2+. Even if there is no explicit lightpen support")
  HardwareHelp_L("for MSX1 computers it can be used with the original " + Chr(34) + "Sanyo Lightpen" + Chr(34) + " cartridge which provides")
  HardwareHelp_L("provides the corresponding firmware (16KB ROM).")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Settings")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("FAFE XSAVE   [10000000 XXXXXXXX]")
  HardwareHelp_L("FB00 YSAVE   [*0000000 YYYYYYYY] *-light pen interrupt request")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("More precisely:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("             +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("FAFE         |  X7 |  X6 |  X5 |  X4 |  X3 |  X2 |  X1 |  X0 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("             +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("FAFF         |  LP | CX6 | CX5 | CX4 | CX3 | CX2 | CX1 | CX0 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("             +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("FB00         |  Y7 |  Y6 |  Y5 |  Y4 |  Y3 |  Y2 |  Y1 |  Y0 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("             +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("FB01         |  0  | CY6 | CY5 | CY4 | CY3 | CY2 | CY1 | CY0 |")
  HardwareHelp_L("             +-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("X0-X7 | Lightpen X position (0-255)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  Updated by PAD(8) in MSX-Basic respectively the corresponding BIOS routines.")
  HardwareHelp_L("X0-X7 | Lightpen Y position (0-211)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  Updated by PAD(8) in MSX-Basic respectively the corresponding BIOS routines.")
  HardwareHelp_L("LP | Lightpen flag")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  1 if lightpen is present, 0 if lightpen is not present.")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  This flag is set during MSX2/2+ boot and not modified later.")
  HardwareHelp_L("CX0-CX6 | Position of left-most pixel (0-127)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  This value is set to 44 ($2C) during MSX2/2+ boot and not modified later.")
  HardwareHelp_L("CY0-CY6 | Position of top-most pixel (0-127)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  This value is set to 38 ($26) during MSX2/2+ boot and not modified later.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Behavior")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("X position is in range 0-255 and Y-position is in range 0-211.")
  HardwareHelp_L("If the lightpen is placed outside of the drawable area")
  HardwareHelp_L("the PAD(8) command still succeeds and resulting coordinates")
  HardwareHelp_L("are automatically adjusted to the best-matching position.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Boot")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("???")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Related")
  HardwareHelp_L("Bit 6 of port &HF5 is be set if a lightpen is available.")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("MSX-BIOS: SANYO LIGHTPEN", "Lightpen (Sanyo compatible)")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("BIT AND BYTE ENCODING")
  HardwareHelp_L("=====================")
  HardwareHelp_L("Information on tape is stored as square waves of 1200 or 2400 baud. The")
  HardwareHelp_L("encoding used is FSK (frequency shifting key). The next table shows the")
  HardwareHelp_L("relation between the selected recording speed, the bits and the pulses")
  HardwareHelp_L("put on tape:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("bps   bit   encoding          freq (Hz)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                    _______")
  HardwareHelp_L("1200   0    _______|       |  1200")
  HardwareHelp_L("                ___     ___")
  HardwareHelp_L("1200   1    ___|   |___|   |  2400")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("                ___")
  HardwareHelp_L("2400   0    ___|   |          2400")
  HardwareHelp_L("              _   _")
  HardwareHelp_L("2400   1    _| |_| |          4800")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Each byte is put on tape as a sequence of 11 bits:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L(" 0 b0 b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6 b7 1 1  (one startbit, 2 stopbits, LSb first)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Futhermore 2 kinds of headers are used: short and long, a long header being")
  HardwareHelp_L("4 times as long as a short one. They consist of a series of square wave pulses")
  HardwareHelp_L("(bit value 1):")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("bps   header   freq (Hz)   nr of pulses ")
  HardwareHelp_L("1200  short    2400        4000             (5/3  sec)")
  HardwareHelp_L("1200  long     2400        16000            (20/3 sec)")
  HardwareHelp_L("2400  short    4800        8000")
  HardwareHelp_L("2400  long     4800        32000")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("FILE FORMATS")
  HardwareHelp_L("============")
  HardwareHelp_L("Files on tape are stored in two blocks:")
  HardwareHelp_L("- the first block is the filedescriptor,")
  HardwareHelp_L("- the second contains the filedata.  ")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Three types of files exist:")
  HardwareHelp_L("  BASIC file, memory dump and data file.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("BASIC File (CSAVE/CLOAD)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  filedescriptor: long header")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  10x #D3       (filetype)")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  6 bytes filename")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("  file data:      short header")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  tokenized BASIC")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  7x #00")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("memory dump (BSAVE/BLOAD)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  filedescriptor: long header")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  10x #D0")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  6 bytes filename")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("  file data:      short header")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  2 bytes start address")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  2 bytes end address")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  2 bytes exec address")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  memory dump")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("data file (SAVE/LOAD/OPEN)")
  HardwareHelp_L("  filedescriptor: long header")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  10x #EA")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  6 bytes filename")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("  file data:      short header")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  block of 256 bytes")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  short header")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  block of 256 bytes")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  ....")
  HardwareHelp_L("                  last block contains 256x #1A (^Z/EoF)")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("MSX BIOS - TAPE Device Routines")
  HardwareHelp_L("are located at #00E1 to #00F3 in the MAIN-ROM.")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("Tape I/O", "Tape I/O")

  HardwareHelp_Begin()
  HardwareHelp_L("The MSX ROM can start in page 1 (&H4000) or page 2 (&H8000) of any slot.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Each ROM has a 16 byte header with the following meaning:")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4000 or 8000 &H41 ('A') Magic ID")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4001 or 8001 &H41 ('B')")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4002 or 8002 Init adress LSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4003 or 8003 Init adress MSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4004 or 8004 Call Statement handler adress LSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4005 or 8005 Call Statement handler adress MSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4006 or 8006 Device handler adress LSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4007 or 8007 Device handler adress MSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4008 or 8008 Basic text adress LSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("4009 or 8009 Basic text adress MSB")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("400A or 800A Reserved")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("400B or 800B ")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("400C or 800C Reserved")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("400D or 800D ")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("400E or 800E Reserved")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("400F or 800F ")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("See also: MILC: Call Cartridge")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Magic ID")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Each valid MSX-ROM is identified by " + Chr(34) + "AB" + Chr(34) + ".")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Init adress")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This routine initializes the ROM-cartridge.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Call Statement handler adress")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This routine handles MSX-Basic CALL statements.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("The name of the CALL statement is stored in PROCNM (&HFD89), where all characters are")
  HardwareHelp_L("automatically converted to uppercase.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Device handler adress")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This routine handles device-extensions, such as " + Chr(34) + "COM:" + Chr(34) + ", " + Chr(34) + "CAS:" + Chr(34) + " or " + Chr(34) + "A:" + Chr(34) + ".")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Recognize device: Entry with A=0FFH indicates that the device is not yet recognized.")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("Perform device operation: Entry with A different from 0FFH indicates that the device")
  HardwareHelp_L("is already known.")
  HardwareHelp_L("Basic text adress")
  HardwareHelp_L("")
  HardwareHelp_L("This is MSX-Basic program text, which can be interpreted.")
  HardwareHelp_Commit("MSX ROM", "ROM (geral)")

EndProcedure
